// GENERATED by tools/extract-event-text.py from the Gloomhaven Secretariat data (gh2e/events.json). Do not edit by hand.
// Event card fronts: n narrative, o options [{l label, t text, r returns to deck}], r card returns to deck.

export const EVENT_FRONT = {
  city: {
    1: {"n":"You're at the University, doing guard duty for a special museum exhibition sponsored by the Merchant's Guild. The artifacts on display were unearthed from an archeological dig near the city of Demonsgate, and the focal piece is an ancient suit of armor made of thousands of links of steel. It's seen better days, but is in good condition for something crafted millennia ago. The placard next to it says that it belonged to an ancient leader with legendary luck who never fell in battle.\n\nThe day passes uneventfully, and your voice is sore by the end from reminding people not to touch the chainmail. The curators pack up the exhibits and give you your payment, wheeling the display out to the courtyard for transit to the next city on the schedule.\n\nYou're about to step out towards the Sleeping Lion for a drink when you spot a steel link on the ground—it must've fallen off the armor you were guarding. You scoop it up and feel the heft in your hand... those ancient warriors weren't fooling around.","o":[{"l":"A","t":"Call after the curators and return it to them."},{"l":"B","t":"It won't be missed. Keep the link for good luck."}]},
    2: {"n":"The difference between rich people and poor people is that rich people have enough money to pay someone else to solve their problems. A wealthy businessman has set his sights on some real estate on the border of the Coin District. Unfortunately for him, it's owned by a particularly obstinate old Savvas who has no intention of giving up its home for a new shop or apothecary or restaurant or whatever dazzling new property the businessman has in mind. The businessman has tried everything and after being refused, again, he's turned to you as a last resort.\n\n\"I'll give you ten gold just to knock on the door and remind it I want to buy the place. Thirty if you can convince that crusty Savvas to take the deal.\" Seems like a good job either way.\n\nYou walk up to the Savvas' door and discuss your plan amongst yourselves.","o":[{"l":"A","t":"Turn on the charm and explain why the businessman's deal is an excellent offer."},{"l":"B","t":"Threaten the Savvas to encourage it to leave."},{"l":"C","t":"Pocket the payment and head back to the Sleeping Lion without knocking."}]},
    3: {"n":"An enthusiastic crowd has gathered around a lone magician in the New Market. She adeptly produces silks and snowbirds from an empty hat, divines a merely thought-of card, and finds coins behind the ears of nearby children. The building audience is delighted by her presentation, clapping and laughing at her jokes.\n\n\"It is time for the main event! Children and the sensitive or infirmed, I invite you to look away... All other viewers, behold!\" She dramatically rips a red curtain off a painted wooden box. The sides are pierced to the hilt by a dozen curved swords, embedded in every direction. She opens a pair of padlocks on the side to reveal the interior: sure enough, blades occupy every available space, leaving no room for much else.\n\nShe begins to draw the swords one by one, demonstrating each is real and solid with a firm twang against the cobblestones.\n\n\"Now, I'll need a full-sized, corporeal volunteer... Quatryls, Vermlings, Aesthers, and Harrowers, you can sit this one out.\"","o":[{"l":"A","t":"Raise your hand (select which party member is volunteering now)."},{"l":"B","t":"Watch the trick closely to see how it's done."}]},
    4: {"n":"It's rare that you're called in for such high-profile work, but you're being paid ten gold each to staff extra protection for a visiting nobleman. The town guard was supposed to be running the security detail, but the nobleman wound up on their bad side after promoting \"rebellious ideologies.\"\n\nYou're making a path through the crowd to allow him to enter the town hall when you spot it: a glimmer of steel at the crest of a nearby rooftop. The unmistakable THUNK of a crossbow confirms the threat—you have just a split second before impact into the chest of your charge.","o":[{"l":"A","t":"Leap in front of the crossbow bolt to save the nobleman."},{"l":"B","t":"Call out a warning."}]},
    5: {"n":"\"Hey, looking for some cash?\" A smarmy-looking Vermling with a mug of ale slides into your booth at the Sleeping Lion. His hair is unnaturally slicked back with a transparent oil, pulling his eyebrows a bit too high.\n\n\"You've been preapproved, just got word this afternoon. Came here straightaway to let you know. You've been looking at some shiny new toys in the shops, no? Some cash could make a big difference in your survivability out there.\"\n\nWary, you ask what the catch is.\n\n\"No catch. Just a loan. You get 50 gold today. You pay back a little interest here and there. You'll barely even notice, you'll have so much extra lying around.\" You inquire about the interest rate, and just who is offering this loan.\n\nThe Vermling clicks his tongue. \"It's a lotta math, beads on the abacus. I would focus on the today, you might not even be around tomorrow. Maybe you die? Maybe you retire? A lot of maybes, I think. Don't worry your head about it.\"","o":[{"l":"A","t":"Accept the money."},{"l":"B","t":"Refuse the loan."}]},
    6: {"n":"Down an alley shortcut, you come across an unusual addition: a Valrath, bound and gagged, lying by the side of the road. You roll him onto his side and his frantic eyes confirm he's still alive. A note strapped to his chest in excellent handwriting reads:\n\n\"To the Town Guards: This man was apprehended removing jewelry from nearby homes. Govern him accordingly.\n\nBy the verdant light of the scarab, The Green Carapace\"\n\nYou see a pair of guards patrolling down the alley in your direction, but it doesn't seem like they've spotted you in the dark yet. The bound man's guttural groans suggests he's trying to say something. You pull down the gag and he wastes no time:\n\n\"That crazed vigilante got me all wrong! You've gotta let me go. I'm—listen. You're like me, right? Just trying to get by in this city. I can help you! I... I've got jewelry. Magic jewelry. You untie me and I'll tell you where it is. It's all yours!\"","o":[{"l":"A","t":"Untie the man."},{"l":"B","t":"Shout for the guards."}]},
    7: {"n":"You're taking a shortcut through Town Hall when an assistant approaches you. \"Excuse me? Could you please assist me? We need an unbiased opinion.\" You follow her into the central rotunda, where you find the council in session.\n\nCouncilwoman Vale furrows her brow at the sight of you. \"Surely there are others that would be better suited to providing insight?\"\n\nCouncilman Greymare interjects: \"It's a fine choice. Mercenaries are the lifeblood of a city, particularly one where the military is demonstrably inferior to the services a sellsword can provide.\" His words trail out like thick barbs against the pro-military councilmembers.\n\n\"So be it,\" Vale continues, ignoring the comment. \"Mercenaries, speak for the populace: is a functional economy more important than security? Does the average citizen of Gloomhaven care more for their pocketbooks or their safety?\" The councilmembers lean in to hear your response.","o":[{"l":"A","t":"The economy is the most important function of government."},{"l":"B","t":"The defense of the city is the most important function of government."}]},
    8: {"n":"\"Which one of you thinks they're all that?\" A drunken Quatryl patron at the Sleeping Lion steps up to your usual table, wavering as he addresses you. His eyes pass in an out of focus, and it's not clear who he means to be accosting.","o":[{"l":"A","t":"Select a character to step up to the challenger, then flip this card.","r":1}]},
    9: {"n":"The Sekhem Gardens are a massive enclosed greenhouse in the Traveler's District. Exotic plants and humid air make for an attraction to all walks of life, but the primary visitors are the Valraths that take comfort in the oasis-like natural beauty of their ancestral home. Temperatures inside are high enough to make even most Valraths sweat, but Councilwoman Vale doesn't show a drop of perspiration as she winds an iridescent blue vine along some trelliswork.\n\n\"I've discovered some unusual legends.\" She expertly weaves it in and out, tracing the glazing of the window. \"Or rather, my daughter did. She spends altogether too much time with her nose in a book.\" The councilwoman snips the end of the vine, tying it to the trellis with a velvet ribbon.\n\n\"I am told of a tower between the Watcher Mountains and the Dagger Forest, that a machine of war there challenges all comers. If it is true, you are to defeat it, dismantle it, and bring it to me. I suspect that, like most knowledge committed to books, it is fanciful hyperbole. But regardless, you will see it investigated.\" She makes it sound much less optional than most people who hire you.","o":[{"l":"A","t":"Ask to keep the machine if you can defeat it."},{"l":"B","t":"Ask for access to the city armory to prepare for the quest."}]},
    10: {"n":"\"Okay, don't freak out. This is a totally consensual thing that is happening, and I need everyone to just stay calm, okay?\" You're standing in the apartment of Xain, a local Orchid barfly who frequents the Sleeping Lion. Also standing in Xain's apartment is a full-sized sun demon, dumping light into the room and crouching to fit under the low ceiling. It quietly watches the proceedings.\n\n\"Mercenaries, meet Sunny. Sunny: these're the best mercenaries at the Sleeping Lion, at least on short notice. Great—hit it, Sunny.\" Sunny picks up a metal paperweight from the bookshelf and compresses it tightly within its brightly-glowing hands. You avert your gaze, but when it's done, you see the paperweight has been reformed into a tiny, perfectly-formed replica of a man-made island with unintelligible contraptions sticking out of it. Xain closes his eyes and touches the display with a fingertip, interpreting the object.\n\n\"Sunny says bugs have taken over some specialty forge built by his kind. Help Sunny clear out the infestation, and it'll use the forge to make you something amazing.\" Xain opens his eyes and looks at you. \"Deal?\"","o":[{"l":"A","t":"Agree to help Sunny clear out the infestation."},{"l":"B","t":"Demand to speak with Sunny alone."}]},
    11: {"n":"Rain tumbles from the sky, driving most of the citizenry to drier confines indoors. You're heading back home from the Sleeping Lion when you see a portly man in a black cloak nearly dragging a limp teen through the alleyways.\n\nThe cloaked man notices you approach and moves to block your view. \"Ah, sorry, my friend took a spill. I will ensure he returns home safely from here. Thank you for your interest, regardless.\" You push him aside easily enough to get a better look, and the teen he's carrying slumps face-down into a puddle. You prod him with the edge of your boot and he stirs, sitting upright and sputtering water. He looks just to the side of you as though he's seeing something that isn't there.\n\nWith uncanny stilted movement, the teen nods to you. \"Yes, thank you for your interest. I'll be returned home safely.\"","o":[{"l":"A","t":"Let the cloaked man return the teen home."},{"l":"B","t":"Refuse to release the teen to the cloaked man."}]},
    12: {"n":"\"This is the greatest invention ever created!\" A Quatryl has set up a display stand on a streetcorner, tapping a black cane on the ground in an effort to draw a crowd. \"Behold!\" With a flourish, she yanks a curtain off a curious, brass-bound box.\n\n\"This marvel—using the exacting powers of science—can induce the feelings of drunkenness without a sip of alcohol or inhalation of smokes of any kind! Tend a bar without spirits, enliven a party without consequence. Gather round, and experience the wonder!\" With that, she reaches up and throws a brass-handled lever on the side of the contraption, and the box gets noisy. The box is noisy. BRRRrr. BRRR! It's noisy—the, the box.\n\nYour near the box and it's noisy and you feel noisy.\n\n\"Oh no, no no—that's not right.\"","o":[{"l":"A","t":"Throw the lever down."},{"l":"B","t":"Throw the lever up."},{"l":"C","t":"Throw up."}]},
    13: {"n":"You hear screams from the south as you approach the docks.\n\nA group of women run toward you in a panic, nearly knocking you over as they race by. One of them shouts: \"An invasion!\"\n\nYou hurry to the docks and find a crowd of workers on edge, armed with makeshift weapons and circling one particular pier.\n\nStanding at the far end of the wooden planks is a group of Lurkers—terrifying crab-like monsters as big as an Inox and equally ferocious. Except these Lurkers don't appear to be hostile. They are simply standing on the dock, clacking their claws in a strange rhythm.","o":[{"l":"A","t":"Raise arms and fight the Lurkers back into the sea."},{"l":"B","t":"Approach the Lurkers cautiously and attempt to communicate with them."}]},
    14: {"n":"You're standing in the cramped room of Pinter Droman, a fledgling Quatryl inventor. Stacked to the ceiling are plans, parts, and pieces of various machines in half-built stages. He wastes no time launching into his issue.\n\n\"I bought this off a Vermling—they're the ones most likely to acquire these things, you see. It's an old ledger showing mining routes. Compare them to what's still in operation today, and there! Right there! This tunnel is on the old maps, but not on the current ones.\" Pinter flips a magnifying glass down from his visor, leaning in close to the paperwork strewn across his desk.\n\n\"But this rock is dense—it's more like what you'd find in the mountains to the north than the ones around here. You couldn't cut into it even with the tools of today. If there really is a mine there, then they used technology that exceeds our own. Imagine what we could build! Gather that for me, would you? I'm sort of between incomes at the moment, but I can compensate you with my inventions.\"","o":[{"l":"A","t":"Accept one of his inventions as payment."},{"l":"B","t":"Ask to browse through his unfinished blueprints."}]},
    15: {"n":"The acrid smell of smoke assaults your senses. The university's southern annex is on fire. Citizens are fleeing every direction in panic, hindering relief efforts. If no one intervenes, it's likely to spread, and the town guard hasn't yet arrived.\n\n\"You're going to do something, right?\" A pair of street-denizens rushes up to you, ready to assist. One is an older gentleman who looks a bit crazed, but his build suggests a strength about him. The other is a meek younger woman who looks quick on her feet. \"Tell us where to go and we'll help!\"","o":[{"l":"A","t":"Distribute your characters, along with the older gentleman and the younger woman, among the following tasks:\nHerding the panicking citizens.\nEntering the burning building to rescue people.\nAttempting to temper the flames.\n\nWhen everyone is assigned to tasks, flip this card over."}]},
    16: {"n":"You spot a member of the Sect, a group of curious individuals who worship demons, picking produce at a shop in the New Market. He's pointing at decadent displays of fruits and honeyed sweets, each of which is piled into his cart by the shop keep. The store owner loads the last of the selections—a crate of plump red radishes—onto the scales for weighing. \"That will be... 10 gold.\"\n\nThe Sect member twists within its clothes and removes a leather satchel of coins, passing it over to the store owner for counting. Before he gets to the exit, though, the store owner speaks up, concerned. \"Hey. Hey! I don't know what this is, but either you pay, or I'll summon the guards.\" You peek inside the satchel and see a variety of dirt-encrusted gold coins, none of which are recognizable as currency. The Sect member looks to the store owner and then to you, pleading.","o":[{"l":"A","t":"Offer to pay for the produce in exchange for the coinage (requires 10 gold)."},{"l":"B","t":"Support the store owner in demanding proper currency."}]},
    17: {"n":"\"Excuse me, mercenaries?\" A silver-cloaked man approaches you, a brooch of fresh flowers pinned to his jacket. The sickly-sweet perfume he carries overwhelms you as he rushes up.\n\n\"There is a crate that will be exiting Gloomhaven momentarily... the gentlemen at the gate will not open it, yes?\"\n\nIs he asking you or telling you? A trickle of black liquid leaks out the side of his mouth, and he dabs it with a black handkerchief. \"If you can assist in its exit, you will be compensated. Well? Compensated.\"\n\nThe guards will give extreme scrutiny to anyone with his odd behavior, but it should be no issue for you to push the crate through as supposed supplies for a mission.","o":[{"l":"A","t":"Help the cloaked man get his crate through the checkpoint."},{"l":"B","t":"Alert the guards to the fishy behavior."}]},
    18: {"n":"\"Perhaps you do not understand, she is the most beautiful girl to have ever walked this earth, and her eyes are like stars sparkling in a summer's breeze! I care not what my father thinks or what her witch of a mother believes—I must wed her! I simply must!\" Greggory Greymare, teenage son of pro-merchant Councilman Greymare, has flopped unceremoniously on a fainting couch in his father's estate. He's called you here to buy your services in delivering a love letter to Nectar Vale, the teenage daughter of pro-military Councilwoman Vale—the archnemesis to his father's dealings. He's prattling on instead of paying you so you can be on your way.\n\n\"I would give all my possessions for a look into her eyes. My father would admonish me, surely, if he knew the depths of my heart. Her own mother would surely forbid our union should she hear the beating of our two chests in time. Will you help me? What would it cost to deliver this simple letter of love to my sweet Nectar? Fifteen gold?\"","o":[{"l":"A","t":"Deliver the note to Nectar Vale."},{"l":"B","t":"Show the note to the boy's father, Councilman Greymare."},{"l":"C","t":"Show the note to the girl's mother, Councilwoman Vale."}]},
    19: {"n":"A group of Merchant's Guild tax collectors are winding through the stalls of the Sinking Market. The central collector is a balding man with a bronze pin on his lapel. He's flanked by two wiry associates wearing well-fitting suits. \"Taxes,\" he states matter-of-factly, a leather ledger in hand.\n\nThe grocer expresses his frustration: \"I just paid you jokers! I can't make a profit with all the nonsense 'taxes' your guild applies.\"\n\nThe tax collector speaks some pre-rehearsed words: \"Taxes today pay for improvements tomorrow. Pay, or hawk your wares outside the protection of the Sinking Market. There's plenty of real estate outside the city.\"\n\n\"Protection? It's the Sinking Market! The whole thing is sinking into the bay! I've moved my stand twice this year!\" The grocer grabs a meat mallet and brandishes it at them. Taken aback, the tax collectors approach you with a proposition: \"Mercenaries, right? Help us collect what's due the merchant guild, and we'll compensate you.\"","o":[{"l":"A","t":"Help the tax collectors recover the taxes they're owed."},{"l":"B","t":"Help the grocer drive off the tax collectors."}]},
    20: {"n":"After a long night helping load a merchant's caravan, you head through the Sinking Market to pick up payment for your efforts. Your employer made clear they were heading out to Jhinda at noon, so if you want the gold before they leave town, you'll need to hurry. You're hustling as best you can when a frail, white-haired woman approaches you and grabs you by the arm. She reeks of grime and sewage, and passersby are turning their noses up in disgust.\n\n\"Rats! So many rats! I don't know where they're coming from, but they're living in my cellar! They ate through three jars of my preserves just yesterday!\" She tugs weakly at your sleeve, which leaves a noticeable dirty mark. \"I've asked dozens of mercenaries, but they've turned me away because I can't pay enough! Please, can you help me?\"\n\nIf you don't find the merchant in the next few minutes you won't get paid for last night's work.","o":[{"l":"A","t":"Tell the woman you can't help her and find the merchant who owes you money."},{"l":"B","t":"Help the woman and forgo collecting payment for the merchant's work."}]},
    21: {"n":"The citizenry know to never cross the painted orange line that circles the Void, a black sand field in the north of Gloomhaven. Anything that crosses the line turns to a coarse black dust in a shower of sparkling lights.\n\nDespite the danger, it's the central component of a celebration unique to the city. Today is the holiday known as the Spectral Jubilee—an evening for the populace to pack shoulder-to-shoulder to sing carols and toss their burdens into the Void. Supposedly, the more meaningful an object you sacrifice, the larger the spectacle of sparkling lights it produces.\n\nTonight is the Spectral Jubilee. If you have something to contribute to the unending darkness, you can do so, but if you came unprepared, there are plenty of vendors ready to make some coin by selling meaningful possessions.","o":[{"l":"A","t":"Select an item of yours now to throw in (you will lose the item)."},{"l":"B","t":"Pay a vendor for a meaningful item of theirs to throw in (requires 15 gold)."},{"l":"C","t":"Enjoy the event but do not participate yourself."}],"r":1},
    22: {"n":"You were supposed to help guard a spice delivery, but a more enticing (and frankly, lucrative) scenario presented itself, and you won't have time. There are plenty of mercenaries looking for coin. Why not hire them to do your work while you take a cut?\n\nThere's the Jaws of the Lion, the best-known mercenary company in Gloomhaven. They also operate out of the Sleeping Lion, but they established their footing before you and can command high prices.\n\nThere's the Sin-Ra Syndicate, a shadowy organization whose calling card is a skull on a spike. They typically run their own nefarious operations, but have been known to accept jobs to keep their coffers full.\n\nOr you could employ the Four Crosses, a crass mercenary company whose leader sports a red mohawk. They're rough around the edges, but it's a simple enough job, even for them.","o":[{"l":"A","t":"Hire the Jaws of the Lion."},{"l":"B","t":"Hire the Sin-Ra Syndicate."},{"l":"C","t":"Hire the Four Crosses."}]},
    23: {"n":"\"Someone's coming, juice it!\" You round the corner of the alley and hear footsteps escaping in the distance.\n\nHeavy rain has turned this backstreet into a series of pockmarked muddy puddles. At your feet is a slumped figure discarded unceremoniously between old crates. You've seen enough dead bodies to recognize the motionless heap in front of you as a recently-deceased Orchid man, a trickle of blood winding its way between the cobblestones beneath him.\n\nThe attackers are long gone, and tracking them in the rain doesn't look promising.","o":[{"l":"A","t":"Loot the body."},{"l":"B","t":"Alert the authorities."}]},
    24: {"n":"A reporter for the Haven Herald has been buying drinks at the Sleeping Lion for a few nights now, clearly hunting for a story. He's well-known for twisting interviews to paint his subjects in a negative light, and the regulars are wary enough to give him nothing.\n\nYou managed to avoid him yesterday, but he's back again this evening and is even more persistent. He sits down at your table.\n\n\"Listen, I know you know who I am. But all I'm looking for is an interview about what the life of a mercenary is really like. You talk with me for ten minutes, and I'll buy your time. That's your job, right? People pay you for your time. How's ten gold sound? Think of it like any other job—but instead of stomping spines I just want you to tell me about the work you do day-to-day. Sound fair?\"\n\nHe pulls out a notebook without waiting for an answer. \"So... who do you work for most of the time?\"","o":[{"l":"A","t":"Tell him you usually work for the Merchants."},{"l":"B","t":"Tell him you usually work for the Military."},{"l":"C","t":"Tell him you usually work for the Demons."},{"l":"D","t":"Tell him you're not interested in talking, even for ten gold."}]},
    25: {"n":"It's the lavish birthday party of Bonecruncher, a local loan shark. Suddenly, an explosion rocks the venue.\n\nYou find Bonecruncher, blood running from a gash on his forehead, shouting at Aerin, Brint, and Cade, his three grown children. The source of the explosion is a smoldering pile of detritus that used to be the gift table. \"One of you brats just tried to kill me! This is why I didn't have more of you runts! Which of you... who...\" He promptly collapses to the floor from the blood loss. An enormous Savvas starts administering medical attention. In the chaos, you pick up the following information:\n\nNeither Aerin nor Cade brought a red gift, and both have a younger sibling.\n\nThe eldest brought a yellow gift.\n\nCade is not the oldest.\n\nBefore you can make sense of it, an Aesther in an unusual hat steps out from behind you. \"Hello, something of a whodunnit. Gripping. The important bit is that each child brought just one gift—a green one, a red one, or a yellow one. The green detonated: find out who brought that, and you have your culprit.\"","o":[{"l":"A","t":"Determine who attempted to kill their father, then flip this card. (Say the name aloud now.)"}]},
    26: {"n":"A Quatryl mail carrier is stopped just inside the gates to Gloomhaven, arguing with the town guard. \"I don't have the mail stamp. I've been on the road for weeks—I had to fight a polar bear to bring this here. Nothing else is going through that pass until the thaw, months from now.\" You step up and ask what the problem is.\n\nThe guard turns to you and explains, if only to reduce the crowd watching the scene unfold. \"She lost her mail's certificate of passage. Without the documentation, she'll need to pay an import tariff equal to the value of the goods in transit.\"\n\n\"You're not going to find some other idiot to make the trek up to Frosthaven to collect the missing paperwork—it's a miracle I made it back at all. It's mail! Look at it! I'm not smuggling thistle!\"\n\n\"I'm sorry, but we'll need to open each of the packages and confirm the value. I'll issue you a receipt, and you can retrieve your belongings at the town hall after the tariff is paid.\"","o":[{"l":"A","t":"Intercede on her behalf."},{"l":"B","t":"Support the guard in following protocol."}]},
    27: {"n":"\"Hey, you want to make some easy cash?\" A man with scarred hands and a weathered look approaches you, casting a cautious look back over his shoulder.\n\n\"I'm new in town, came in from White Oak a few hours ago. My wagon broke down and I just need to get enough money for repairs to get back. I've got some personal belongings I'm looking to offload, but my reputation with the shops around here isn't as good as back home, so they're lowballing me. Could you sell this stuff for me? I'll give you a cut.\"","o":[{"l":"A","t":"Sell the man's goods for him.","r":1},{"l":"B","t":"Report him to the authorities.","r":1}]},
    28: {"n":"Pacing the floor of his estate before you is Councilman Greymare. The urgent invitation you received didn't have any details, but you're not one to turn down a chance to visit Gloomhaven leadership. He beckons you over.\n\n\"Hurry, everything is falling apart!\" As if to punctuate his statement, you hear the shout of a woman from the room behind him, followed by a thunderous crash. The councilman gives a sigh before continuing.\n\n\"I have an emissary here from the Eastern Continent. Their delegation has been staying under my roof, in my care. This afternoon was supposed to be the presentation of a gift of rare star iron.\" He wrings his hands as another loud crash can be heard from the room.\n\n\"Perhaps you are familiar with Gloomhaven's very own Harrower vigilante, the Green Carapace?\" You've heard the name. \"A half hour ago, they broke into my parlor and stole the chest of star iron! And in the process, they attacked the esteemed delegate, reducing them to...\" The councilman spots something crawling out from under the door and instinctively goes to smash it, stopping himself at the last moment. He ushers it carefully back inside.\n\n\"Well, the vigilante dissolved the delegate into a sea of bugs.\"\n\n\"Perhaps that's a bit dramatic. The esteemed delegate Buzar is a Harrower, so they're normally made of bugs. They're just normally bugs that can... talk.\" Councilman Greymare cracks the door open to give you a look inside at the horrifying sight: thousands of bugs crawl across every visible surface. A fainting couch features most of a Harrower, a sentient hive-creature made of insects, but it's clear they're not in control of most of their body. Buzzing through the air and draped all over the cushions is a black carpet of undulating legs and mandibles. An Orchid woman in the traditional garb of the Eastern Continent rushes about, trying to corral the insects.\n\nHe closes the door, visibly disturbed by the sight. \"Where is my valet? We need more containers.\" He tugs on a nearby closet door but is thwarted by the lock. \"Blast it. Where is Apollon!?\" He starts down the hall when the closet door clicks open from the inside, and a slim, olive-skinned man stumbles out and falls to the ground.\n\n\"Ugh... the Green... Carapace, they attacked me. I tried to stop them...\" Apollon gives a ragged cough, and from the way his wrist is hanging, it looks broken.\n\nThe councilman looks mortified. \"Not you too! The chest of star iron is set to be presented in two hours' time—that gift will secure our trade route for the next decade! I've got the house cordoned off, the chest can't have left the grounds. Find it before then, or this will be a diplomatic nightmare!\"","o":[{"l":"A","t":"Interview Apollon, the battered valet."},{"l":"B","t":"Interview Buzar, the incapacitated diplomat."}]},
    29: {"n":"\"Fill the gauntlet! Fill the gauntlet!\" A guardsman is approaching passersby and asking for donations to build upgrades on the eastern barracks. He holds out an old iron gauntlet and generous (or perhaps, sufficiently irritated) citizens drop a few coins in as they pass.\n\n\"You there! Would you care to help fill the gauntlet? A proper barracks is an important part of protecting the city!\"","o":[{"l":"A","t":"Donate a few coins.","r":1},{"l":"B","t":"Politely refuse to support the town guard.","r":1},{"l":"C","t":"Aggressively and loudly refuse to support the town guard.","r":1}]},
    30: {"n":"It's well past midnight as you finally make your way home from your last adventure. On the way to a warm bed, you hear whimpering emanating from a side street and go closer to investigate.\n\n\"Two of us, one of you! That's two counts of resisting arrest! Whaddoyou think that adds up to?\" A pair of guards have cornered an unfortunate Vermling in an alley. With a chuckle, the guard continues, \"I'd guess fifteen years in the Ghost Fortress, were we to turn you in. So, instead, why don't you just give us what's on you, and we'll forget all about this.\"\n\nThe Vermling tries to squeak out a reply. \"I didn't take anything!\"\n\n\"You took this alley tonight, that's enough,\" the guard replies with a laugh. It's cut short as they suddenly realize you're standing there. He instantly softens. \"Hey, listen, let this one slide, and we'll put in a good word with the boss.\"","o":[{"l":"A","t":"These two are a disgrace to the military. Turn them in to their commanding officer."},{"l":"B","t":"No sense in tarnishing the guards' good names over a momentary mistake. Turn a blind eye."}]},
    31: {"n":"The Void is a troubling area in Gloomhaven—a vast, barren expanse of black sand amid the bustling streets of the city. No one enters the painted safety outline around its perimeter.\n\nOn a trip to the University, however, you see a young girl playing past the warning line near the edge of the Void. Before you can warn her, she trips and falls onto the sand.\n\nShe immediately emits a blood-curdling scream and begins to tear at her skin.","o":[{"l":"A","t":"Run to the girl and attempt to pull her out of the Void."},{"l":"B","t":"Watch and make note of the Void's effects on a living creature."}]},
    32: {"n":"You decide to spend your evening slumming it at the Brown Door, a dive bar in the Old Docks. Sitting at the back of the room, you see an old man hunched over a set of glass beads. He somberly invites you to sit at his table, eager for the company. He pours some beads into his fist, placing the rest carefully into his breast pocket.\n\n\"Care to guess how many beads I have in this hand here? Get it right and I'll make it worth your while. It's tougher than it looks!\"\n\nHe laughs, nodding to himself as though he's said something funny. \"It used to be simpler. Simpler, yes.\"","o":[{"l":"A","t":"Collectively guess a number between one and five. (Say the number aloud now.)"}]},
    33: {"n":"Do not allow anyone else to see this card.","o":[{"l":"A","t":"Read section 50.1."}]},
    34: {"n":"You spot a young man just outside the Temple of the Great Oak carrying a small parcel under his arm. He approaches a few nearby groups and walks off dejected. He catches your gaze and moves your way.\n\n\"Excuse me, I'm supposed to deliver this to someone affiliated with the Merchant's Guild. The directions weren't ideal... or, really, directions at all. You wouldn't happen to know where to find them, would you?\"","o":[{"l":"A","t":"Why yes, that's us!"},{"l":"B","t":"Admit you're not in good favor with the Merchant's Guild."}]},
    35: {"n":"One of the local barflies stumbles over to your table, a semi-drunk stupor over her face. Words tumble thoughtlessly out of her mouth.\n\n\"Did you know that I have a glass eye?\" She loudly exclaims to no one in particular. She leans down to give you a better look, and sure enough, her right eye has a clear glassy sheen. Now that you've noticed it, you can't unsee it.\n\n\"It's a really good one. You don't even know which one it is, huh?\" If you weren't sure before, her bloodshot real left eye gives a bit of a twitch and removes all doubt. She grabs your arm, helping you to extend a finger and dragging it closer to her face. \"Poke it. Poke which one you think it is! You'll never get it right.\" She stares directly at your fingertip.","o":[{"l":"A","t":"Poke her in the left eye."},{"l":"B","t":"Poke her in the right eye."}]},
    36: {"n":"Sometimes people pay a mercenary for their strength, and sometimes they pay for their discretion. You've picked up a simple job to ferry a sealed envelope from the Boiler District to the Sinking Market, and you asked exactly the right number of questions: none.\n\nThey said if you prove yourself here, there's more work in the future. Fifteen gold for an hour's work? Easy.","o":[{"l":"A","t":"Deliver the envelope to the address as agreed.","r":1},{"l":"B","t":"People don't pay like this to deliver love letters. Open the envelope.","r":1}]},
    37: {"n":"You meet with a group of Quatryls, Inox, and Vermlings at the University researching the power core you recovered from the arcane library's construct. Probes run across the shiny object, leaded lamps flickering various indicators as they run one experiment after another.\n\n\"We're close to a breakthrough, I can feel it.\" The lead researcher looks like they believe it. \"We'll just need a little more time, or a little more... investment.\" A loud POP sounds from the device nearest the power core, followed by a stream of soot-laden smoke. You see the researcher is getting antsy. \"We've gone through considerable resources trying to power it on again, and resupply has been difficult.\"\n\nThe researcher shakes her head, walking back over to the testing. \"Good news, though: have I told you that Councilwoman Vale has agreed to let you test the weapon once it's complete?\"","o":[{"l":"A","t":"Encourage Councilwoman Vale to increase the researchers' funding.","r":1},{"l":"B","t":"Pay for the needed parts yourself.","r":1}]},
    38: {"n":"Bonecruncher, the local loan shark, doesn't offer much in the way of hospitality when you visit him in a ramshackle building in the Boiler District. Occupying the only remaining chair in the room is a massive Savvas bodyguard that feels so unthreatened by your presence, it's reading a newspaper. Bonecruncher rips a mouthful of meat off a bone, throwing the remainder to a pair of vicious-looking dogs.\n\n\"I recently got a cargo ship from someone who won't be using it anymore.\" Bonecruncher doesn't elaborate, picking a hunk of meat from between his teeth with a long fingernail. \"But I have a problem: my shipments keep getting raided by enterprising pirates. So, I have a proposition for you. Get on board. Keep my next delivery safe. Do this for me, and I'll give you 150 gold, each.\"\n\nYou're surprised at Bonecruncher's sudden generosity until he continues, \"...in the form of forgiving you and that Sawbones friend of yours' debt to me.\"","o":[{"l":"A","t":"Accept Bonecruncher's offer to clear the debt."},{"l":"B","t":"Demand he pay for your services in gold, same as anyone else."}]},
    39: {"n":"The shrill screams of the populace were a bad sign. The giant drake perched on top of the library was confirmation this was going to be a rough afternoon.\n\n\"I have not killed a human in many hours now, you have nothing to fear!\" Those who can run break into a dead sprint, others take cover behind whatever buildings they can find. \"I am looking for a group of polite mercenaries, perhaps you can—oh please, stop that incessant screaming! I said QUIEEEET!\" A gout of flame bursts from his mouth for emphasis, although you note it wasn't directed at anyone intentionally.\n\nHe spots you amidst the commotion. \"Ah, there you are! I thought I might find you in the perceived safety of your walled city. Obviously not much good against, well...\" he raises his wings and gives a light shrug. \"In any case, I am calling in that favor. I have come to request a meal, at your expense.\" Someone screams in horror.\n\n\"No, I don't mean you.\" The drake narrows his eyes then raises a brow, suddenly unsure. \"Unless...? Is that a customary meal in this place?\"","o":[{"l":"A","t":"Bring the drake to a meal in the Traveler's District."},{"l":"B","t":"Bring the drake to the Sleeping Lion."}]},
    40: {"n":"\"All rise!\"\n\nThe chamber door opens, and in funnel three ivory-masked arbiters. Their identities are well-known—they're borderline celebrities in the public sphere—but tradition dictates their faces are hidden while meting out justice. It's the day of your hearing, and you've arrived early in the hope of getting in the arbiters' good graces to talk your way out of your criminal accusations. It doesn't work well.\n\n\"I recognize you...\" the central arbiter leans over the podium. Their demeanor suggests they don't mean it in a good way.\n\nThe bailiff shouts over the din of the crowd: \"Justice will commence momentarily. Please be seated. If you are among the accused, prepare yourselves accordingly. May your truth fear no open flame.\"\n\nPRIOR OFFENSE: [ ] If this box is checked, read section 120.2.\n\nOTHERWISE: Choose an option as normal.","o":[{"l":"A","t":"Hire a representative to act as your counsel before the arbiters (requires 10 collective gold)."},{"l":"B","t":"Represent yourself before the arbiters."}]},
    41: {"n":"You're staffing the high-profile wedding of Nectar Vale to Greggory Greymare. As the children of councilmembers, this is the event of the year. You're here to check invites at the door and keep the undesirables out. They've even provided clean white gloves to wear... fancy.\n\nBasseloff, the pompous Quatryl bard who has somehow garnered a following, shows up and presents his invite. \"The pleasure is all yours, now let me through.\"","o":[{"l":"A","t":"Allow him to enter."},{"l":"B","t":"Refuse him entry."}]},
    42: {"n":"The next guest is a young woman with an elegant, form-fitting velvet dress. She ascends the staircase to the venue and looks to you to remove the leather rope to allow her inside. \"Hi, I'm here for the Vale wedding?\" You ask for her invitation.\n\n\"Oh, sure! Yes, I have it here.\" She thumbs through her purse and produces it.\n\n\"Looks like it'll be a fun evening - you must see all sorts of celebrities coming through here!\"","o":[{"l":"A","t":"Allow her to enter."},{"l":"B","t":"Refuse her entry."}]},
    43: {"n":"\"Hey, aren't you the mercenaries who took a crossbow bolt to the chest to protect that nobleman?\"\n\nA dock worker looms over your table in the Sleeping Lion, pointing at you with wonder in his eyes. \"Is it true the doctors said you'd never lift a sword again?\"\n\nMore people stop and stare at you. This has become a common occurrence since the nobleman was saved, who has seemingly spent every waking minute telling anyone who will listen about the mercenary that bravely stopped his assassination. You invite the gathering crowd to pull up a chair and hear your retelling of the tale.","o":[{"l":"A","t":"Remind the crowd that it was quite a traumatic incident, and a drink would be helpful, if they'd be so kind. (Requires hero or friend-of-the-hero).","r":1},{"l":"B","t":"Explain they must be mistaking you for someone else."}]},
    44: {"n":"Waiting outside the Sleeping Lion, you find two gruff enforcers. \"Glad we ran into each other, Bonecruncher has been asking about you. Time for a payment on that debt of yours, huh?\" One of the enforcers casually brushes his hair back, tying it up with a length of thin chain in preparation for your response.\n\n\"You didn't forget about your debt, did you? Don't worry, Bonecruncher's got a real good memory, he won't let it slip.\" He cracks his knuckles menacingly. \"Pay up.\"","o":[{"l":"A","t":"Attempt to avoid paying."},{"l":"B","t":"Pay interest on your debt."}]},
    45: {"n":"You come back to your room to find your door knocked off its hinges, and all your personal belongings scattered around the floor.\n\n\"Cozy, I like it.\" Perched on top of your bedsheets is a massive mountain of a Savvas, rocky torso and arms flexing as it stands to greet you. A swirl of red flame idly flickers in its chest, demonstrating this Savvas has complete mastery of fire. With a body that titanic, you don't know what need of magic it would have.\n\n\"Bonecruncher told me you didn't cooperate with repayment.\" The Savvas surveys your room with mild curiosity. \"When people pay, the system works. If people don't pay, the system breaks down. This is just a reminder to cooperate.\" It snaps its fingers, and your bed ignites with a gout of flame. You rush to put it out before it spreads and when you turn back, the Savvas has already gone. Attempts to find the Savvas prove fruitless.","o":[{"l":"A","t":"Report this to the authorities."},{"l":"B","t":"Reinforce your door.","r":1}]},
    46: {"n":"A light knock at your door reveals Apollon, Councilman Greymare's attendant. His arm is bound in a sling, resting uselessly against his side.\n\n\"Mercenaries. Might I come in? The night air is cold, and it carries words to curious ears.\" You oblige his request. When you last saw Apollon, you had identified him as the Green Carapace, a masked vigilante, and were kind enough to keep his secret.\n\n\"Our mutual friend has discovered some unsettling activity near the docks. It's important that someone address it immediately to prevent any further loss of life. Unfortunately, I'm in no condition to attend to it myself right now.\" He uses his good arm to gesture to the sling, the bones still recovering from his fight with Buzar. \"Given the limited pool of people aware of... ahem, I was hoping that you could make an appearance where our friend cannot.\" He jingles a stout bag of coins. \"Surely you have time to spread the verdant light of the scarab.\"","o":[{"l":"A","t":"Ask that he throws in a training session with his \"friend.\""},{"l":"B","t":"Negotiate an additional fee for maintaining his secret."}]},
    47: {"n":"First Shield Harmon wraps up the briefing outside a suspected gang hideout in the Sinking Market: \"Our intel says there's crates inside: one is full of stolen gold, the others rigged to explode. Secure the room and we'll determine which is which.\" On their signal, you push inside.\n\nYou notice the door is booby-trapped a second too late, detonating a charge that ignites the room. The other guards fall back, but through the fire you spot them: four crates, each identical. One holds gold. The others, more explosives.\n\nWith no time for analysis, you select one of the crates at random and push through the growing flames towards it. Before you can reach it, though, a ceiling beam tumbles down, crushing two of the other crates. The damage triggers explosives inside them, further destabilizing the building. There are just two crates left, and fire is licking at your heels. You'll only have time to open one.","o":[{"l":"A","t":"Open the first crate you selected."},{"l":"B","t":"Switch to the other crate."}]},
    48: {"n":"\"Thank you, thank you for coming so quickly.\" The nobleman looks around to confirm the street is empty before inviting you inside. There, in the middle of the room, is the corpse of a thief, a pool of blood staining the rug below.\n\n\"He broke in, I was terrified. I got the drop on him, but it could have readily gone the other way.\" The nobleman looks pretty shaken by the encounter, getting a far-off look. He quickly snaps himself out of it, continuing, \"In any case, it would be politically inconvenient for this to get out. I'm sure this is something you encounter frequently, given your sort of work. Can you help me?\"","o":[{"l":"A","t":"Call in your contacts to remove the body."},{"l":"B","t":"Take care of its removal yourself."}]},
    49: {"n":"The Merchant's Guild tax collectors are winding through the stalls of the Sinking Market. The central collector is a young man with a brass pin on his lapel. He's flanked by two other disheveled associates, each wearing ill-fitting suits. \"Taxes,\" he states matter-of-factly, a brand-new leather ledger in hand.\n\nThe frustrated grocer starts yelling: \"I just paid you idiots! You don't help merchants—you're just bill collectors now!\"\n\nThe tax collector speaks some pre-rehearsed words: \"Taxes today pay for improvements tomorrow. Pay, or hawk your wares outside the protection of the Sinking Market. There's plenty of real estate outside the city walls.\"\n\n\"Protection? It's the Sinking Market! It's sinking into the bay! I've moved my stand twice this year!\" The grocer grabs a meat mallet and brandishes it at them. Taken aback, the tax collectors approach with a proposition: \"Mercenaries, right? Help us collect what's due the merchant guild, and we'll compensate you for your time.\"","o":[{"l":"A","t":"Help the tax collectors recover the taxes they're owed."},{"l":"B","t":"Help the grocer drive off the tax collectors."}]},
    50: {"n":"The shopkeep addresses you with urgency the second you walk into a high-class establishment in the Coin District. \"Sorry, excuse me. There is some business I must attend to across town—would you be willing to man my shop for a few hours? I'll reward you handsomely.\" Without other plans, you shrug and agree.\n\nHours later, you have a newfound appreciation for just how often someone walks through, touches everything, and buys nothing. Finally, the door opens to reveal four mercenaries you recognize as the Four Crosses, a rival group that operates out of the Brown Door, a seedy pub in the Old Docks. The leader is a red-haired brawler with a mohawk who has always rubbed you the wrong way.\n\n\"Whoa—aren't you those mercenaries from the Sleepy Lion? You so poor you have to run a shop between gigs?\" His friends chortle, but before you can respond he spots an ornate battle axe on display. \"That. I want that. How much?\"\n\nYou happen to know it's 20 gold.","o":[{"l":"A","t":"Tell him it's 20 gold."},{"l":"B","t":"Tell him it's 40 gold."}]},
    51: {"n":"\"Help!\" A young soldier stumbles out of a darkened alley, whimpering as a pair of Orchids seize him and drag him back into the shadows. You dash into the alley, where the Orchids have the soldier pinned against the stone wall. All three of them wear pendants in the shape of a trident rising through a spiral—the symbol of the Sect.\n\n\"Please,\" the guard cries, \"They're gonna kill me!\"\n\nOne of the Orchids growls. \"He joined the Sect. We told him our plans, showed him our hideouts, taught him our secret codes...\"\n\n\"And then he tried to run,\" the other Orchid finishes, punching the soldier in the gut. \"We can't let him go and spill everything to the military.\"\n\n\"The Sect is crazy! They're a cult!\" the soldier pleads, \"I didn't know what I was getting into. Please, I swear by the Oak I'll never tell—OOF!\" He coughs as the Orchid lands another blow.","o":[{"l":"A","t":"Explain to the deserter that it's too late to back out now."},{"l":"B","t":"Explain to the Sect enforcers that it's not too late to keep their teeth—if they let the kid go."}]},
    52: {"n":"\"The Sinking Market is sinking.\" A white-and-brown Vermling stands in your path as you travel north to the Boiler's District. She speaks with authority, wearing a rough robe and a long, drooping necklace made of copper wire and thick-braided horsetail. You tell her that the Sinking Market is supposed to be sinking—that's why it's in the name—and there's nothing you could do about it anyway.\n\nBut the Vermling shakes her head. She explains that large, deep pits have begun to suddenly open along the market, swallowing shoppers and even entire merchants' stalls. \"We hear a grinding and a rumble from below, and then the earth swallows us,\" the Vermling says. \"We from the gutters can't pay, but you must help.\"","o":[{"l":"A","t":"Agree to help and descend into one of the sinkholes to investigate."},{"l":"B","t":"Get the city guards to do it instead."}]},
    53: {"n":"The alarm bells blare across the watchtowers, and the citizenry look around in confusion. Before you can assess, a panting town guard recruit on crutches comes rushing up to you. \"True Shields! True Shields!\" He gulps air between sentences.\n\n\"It is you, thank the Oak! First Shield Harmon and Spymaster Argeise and... well, basically everyone are out on annual training. And—and we're under attack from Inox warbands! It's such terrible timing!\"\n\nYou suspect this is no mere coincidence... the Inox you've met in the wilds are cunning enough to know exactly when Gloomhaven will be at its most vulnerable.\n\n\"I saw what your group did at the Battle for Gloomhaven. Can you assume command of the troops? Without your help, I fear we'll be overrun!\"","o":[{"l":"A","t":"Yes, we'll lead the remaining army."},{"l":"B","t":"Surely the army can do the one thing it's supposed to do on its own. Refuse to help."}]},
    54: {"n":"You're walking in the Sinking Market looking for possible work when you're approached by a dirty-looking boy. He's given you leads in the past on a number of mercenary jobs.\n\n\"I need some help, and I really don't know who else to turn to,\" he begins. \"All of us here in the Sinking Market are in a bad way. Our houses are collapsing, our water is foul, and we don't even have enough money for food.\n\n\"We're dying here, and the city is doing nothing to help us. You have friends in high places in the Merchant's Guild, right? They own this whole area. Please, there has to be someone you can talk to.\"","o":[{"l":"A","t":"Talk to the highest ranking people you know on the Merchant's Guild about revitalizing the area."},{"l":"B","t":"Offer to put the boy up in better accommodations on your own dime."}]},
    55: {"n":"You're passing through the Sinking Market when you see a stallworker point you out at a distance to some inquiring guards. The group rushes over to confront you.\n\n\"You there! Halt! Lay down your weapons.\" The guards look visibly jittery, and one thumbs the pommel of his sword, looking for an excuse to draw it. \"By order of the town guard, you're under arrest for conspiracy against Gloomhaven, sedition, and accusations of treason.\"\n\nGreat. Trumped up charges thanks to not playing by the town guard's rules.","o":[{"l":"A","t":"A guard's only as loyal as his last paycheck. Pay off the guards."},{"l":"B","t":"Follow the guard's instructions."},{"l":"C","t":"Run for it."}]},
    56: {"n":"You're walking down an alley following a successful evening at the Sleeping Lion when you spot him: a figure shrouded in darkness blocking your path. The Quatryl calls out an ominous greeting in a haunting tone:\n\n\"There is no justice in this world but that borne from power. The Merchant's Guild sought your aid, and you spurned it. You have displeased power, and it is lashing out, with me as its imperfect instrument.\"\n\nHe clicks a device strapped to his wrist and the alleyway crackles with blue energy. In long, impossible steps, the figure launches forward, vanishing mid-stride only to appear again and again, hundreds of times, as a kaleidoscope of himself. The sequence is impossible to track, each step an eruption of blue lightning. In a final flash he is upon you, twin daggers drawn and angled at your throat.","o":[{"l":"A","t":"Fight the Merchant's Guild bounty hunter.","r":1},{"l":"B","t":"Attempt to reason with the Merchant's Guild bounty hunter."}]},
    57: {"n":"Ever since the new leadership was installed, there have been grumblings. Sure, the streets are safer. There's a quiet peace, and no one is willing to speak up for fear of toppling the precarious instability it rests on. Crime is down, and most folks are indoors before dark. But there are grumblings.\n\nYou're contributing today's wages to the Sleeping Lion when you overhear them: newcomers talking in hushed tones at the next table. It's only snippets, but you're certain you hear the words \"silent\" and \"rebellion,\" followed by some furtive glances around the room. No one else seems to have noticed.","o":[{"l":"A","t":"Make it clear that speaking up against the leadership isn't welcome here."},{"l":"B","t":"Present yourself as sympathetic to their plight."}]},
    58: {"n":"It's taken weeks of effort and trust building, but you've finally ingrained yourself in the resistance cell plotting to strike back at the government. You settle into your seats in a basement under a bakery as the leader, a Valrath by the name of \"Loop,\" addresses the rag-tag gathering before him.\n\n\"We have lived under the yoke of oppression, docile cogs in a machine that exists to grind us to our bones and discard the dust. Our masters care not whether we live or die, merely that we comply. No more.\" The speaker looks to be barely out of his teenage years, but speaks with an impassioned fury.\n\n\"There is no value to life except that which is put towards a worthy cause. For some, our choices today will call for payment on those lives, but know that for each of us that falls, it will be a life well spent. We are the resistance. We are the silent, and the silent have a voice. We will reclaim Gloomhaven for all who come after us! Sleep well, we strike at dawn.\"","o":[{"l":"A","t":"Rise up against the oppressive leadership of Gloomhaven."},{"l":"B","t":"Betray the resistance to the city's rulers."}]},
    59: {"n":"You've returned to the cramped basement below a local bakery that has been taken over by the self-styled \"silent resistance,\" a group of Gloomhaven citizens that are rising up against the government. Before you sits Loop, a young Valrath with piercing gold eyes and a charisma that motivates the people to action.\n\n\"They have the money, the men, the power. But we have righteousness. We have chaos. And we have you.\" Loop gestures at a map of Gloomhaven on the wall.\n\n\"There is a weak point in the lifeblood of Gloomhaven. Here.\" His finger rests on the Silent Bridge, the one path across the Still River in Gloomhaven. \"This bridge allows passage for the caravans from the capital and from all of civilization reach the wealthy of Gloomhaven. Without it, there is no route over the Still River for several days ride—the river is our greatest silent ally. But I recognize a mercenary exists to be paid: how can I reward you for your assistance in destroying the Silent Bridge?\"","o":[{"l":"A","t":"Ask for payment, up front, in gold."},{"l":"B","t":"Refuse payment for the mission."}]},
    60: {"n":"There was no overt, sweeping change from blowing up the Silent Bridge—a few days of chaos before reconstruction efforts began in earnest, but the difference amongst the commoners is electric. The leadership has been put in their place, and the real power of the populace has made itself known.\n\nYou step into the cramped bakery basement that houses the Silent, a rebellious group who you assisted in taking out the bridge and making the people's will known. Loop, the charismatic young Valrath that organized the effort, welcomes you with a smile and a wince. His hand remains bundled with gauze bandages, a casualty of the mission that doctors suggest will never fully heal.\n\n\"Donations continue to come, many from unlikely sources. We have more than we can spend. How would you recommend we apply our meager earnings?\"","o":[{"l":"A","t":"Give it to us."},{"l":"B","t":"Distribute it to the unfortunate."}]},
    61: {"n":"\"That's close enough.\" At just past midnight, you find yourself in an alley behind the Sleeping Lion. A hooded figure is several paces away, conveniently standing in shadow despite the moonlight. An unsigned note inexplicably appeared at your table an hour ago, addressed to \"Valiant Heroes,\" and leading you here.\n\n\"Councilman Myte. He thinks he's above the law.\" The voice sounds strained, as though he's trying to mask his speech with a veil of baritone. Still, it sounds oddly familiar. \"But there is evidence, if you know where to look. If you know the right questions.\" The figure points to a twine-wrapped package leaning against the wall, which you gingerly pick up.\n\n\"He uses them, then they disappear. You've done well for this city. There are things I know. Things I see. But I cannot be in all places. Read the package. Wield this, and govern him accordingly. I will try to get you more when I can.\" With that, he recedes into the shadows, heels clicking away against the cobblestones.","o":[{"l":"A","t":"Let the mysterious informant depart."},{"l":"B","t":"Pursue and unmask the mysterious informant."}]},
    62: {"n":"After an hour of waiting in a well-appointed, overly perfumed lobby, you are invited into the working quarters of Councilman Myte. You draw the doors closed behind you as you enter, sinking into the soft kivak-leather seats. The office is warm, a pleasant fire roaring in a too-large fireplace at the side of the room.\n\n\"Please excuse the delay, my intern is new and failed to alert me. To what do I owe the pleasure of such company today? I am always willing to meet with my constituents.\" The plump Orchid smiles a charming smile that sends a shiver through you.\n\nYou spread the logbook showcasing the disappearances of his interns on his desk, thumbing through the pages. The color drains from his face. \"Where did you get this?\" He sneers, hate bubbling through him. \"No—never mind. What do you want? I'll pay anything for it.\"","o":[{"l":"A","t":"Sell it to him for 30 gold."},{"l":"B","t":"Sell it to him for a favor for your preferred faction."},{"l":"C","t":"Refuse to sell it to him—you merely want him to know you have it."}]},
    63: {"n":"You've just come through the city gates from your latest excursion when a cautious figure starts walking in lockstep with you.\n\n\"Psst! Hey! I hear you've got something of interest on Councilman Myte. I'm a reporter for the Haven Herald.\"\n\nYou feign not having heard her and continue on your way—no point in leaking something to the press before you can get to the bottom of it yourself.\n\n\"Hey. Hey!\" She jogs alongside you to keep up with your brisk pace. \"The people have a right to know who is representing them. I've been tracking this story for weeks and all I need is the smallest bit of evidence—heck, even anonymously—so I can break this wide open. I think he's been killing his interns. Don't they deserve justice? It'll never be traced back to you.\"\n\nShe stops, letting you get a few feet away, then follows up with her best and final argument: \"I can pay.\"","o":[{"l":"A","t":"Accept 25 gold in exchange for the logbook of evidence."},{"l":"B","t":"Refuse the reporter."}]},
    64: {"n":"\"Come in, friends! It's time.\" Your old friend-turned-councilmember invites you into their office. The room is well-appointed in a style befitting the former mercenary. \"Help yourselves to anything in the cabinet, the imported Darkwater is quite nice.\"\n\nYou find your way over to a set of chairs, and your old friend leans in close.\n\n\"I've been doing some digging on my fellow Councilman Myte. He is scum—murderous scum, I'd wager. I've had evidence, but I haven't been able to definitively prove any of it in a way he couldn't flex his political power to get out of... until now. I've spent hundreds in gold tracking his movement, bribing anyone who ever interacted with the man. I think I know where the bodies are buried. Quite literally.\" You can't help but feel this sort of skullduggery truly suits the new councilmember.\n\n\"Political power is useful, but I've reached the point where I need the strength of a mercenary to finish this. I can't get my hands dirty anymore, but I could think of no one I trust more than you.\"","o":[{"l":"A","t":"Agree to help your old friend take down Councilman Myte."},{"l":"B","t":"It's been a while, so remind the esteemed councilmember that mercenaries don't work for free."}]},
    65: {"n":"You're heading out the city gates when you're pushed aside by some gruff-looking guards. They're cordoning off a pathway for some returning high-ranking official coming into town, and for security reasons, they won't be able to let you through until the VIP passes.\n\nAfter a few minutes delay, you finally catch a glimpse: to your great surprise, it's none other than your old mercenary-friend-turned-politician. You wave and after security confirms the connection, you're granted an in-person audience.\n\n\"Friends! How is mercenary life treating you?\" You recount a few quick anecdotes as aides and citizens stream past, each earning a nod or a handshake from the new councilmember. After a few moments, they drop the political veneer and it's like you're talking to your old friend again. \"Tell me—I've had some difficulty really getting a handle on how Gloomhaven is faring. What's the word from the streets?\"","o":[{"l":"A","t":"Tell the councilmember that life for the average citizen is hard."},{"l":"B","t":"Tell the councilmember that taxes are too high, and it's stifling shop sales."}]},
    66: {"n":"You head to the Sinking Market, hoping to find some cheap supplies, when you are surprised to see a crowd gathered in the distance. You push your way through and see a host of dead bodies in the street, covered in insects and strange pustules on their skin.\n\nNearby, a woman screams in terror and runs away. Other more curious onlookers inch closer, trying to get a better view of the grotesque scene.","o":[{"l":"A","t":"Help dispose of the corpses as quickly as possible."},{"l":"B","t":"Investigate the corpses."}]},
    67: {"n":"You enter the grand foyer of the Wayward Inn to visit your old friend-turned-Innkeeper, but are met with an unfamiliar face at the reception desk. A well-dressed Vermling sits cross-legged, a pen in hand as she completes some paperwork. She greets you with a smile as you approach.\n\n\"Oh, hello! Welcome to the newest—and finest—establishment in Gloomhaven. Do you have a reservation?\" You explain that you're just there to visit the innkeeper, who you used to adventure with.\n\n\"Oh! You must be the mercenaries! Boss hasn't been around the last few days. Went to go convince the people at Crossroads Station to send people our way instead of to those big, established inns.\" With a snap of her fingers, she pulls a sealed envelope from under the desk. \"Boss did say to give you this if you came around.\" You tear open the envelope.\n\n\"Went to Crossroads Station. Something's wrong out there. If you're reading this, it went bad. Come find me.\"","o":[{"l":"A","t":"Tell the Vermling concierge what's going on."},{"l":"B","t":"Don't alarm the Vermling concierge with your concerns."}]},
    68: {"n":"In the middle of an intersection stands an older gentleman, frantically pawing through maps of the area, a heavy canvas bag on his hip. He finally decides on a direction, only to stop a few steps in to re-evaluate. He's getting nowhere, and none of the passersby seem interested in getting tangled up with whatever is troubling him.\n\nHe makes eye contact with you and tries again. \"Excuse me, my delegation is arriving soon and I am supposed to secure housing. I was told to go to the Wayward Inn in the Traveler's District, but I can't find it for the life of me. Could you give me directions?\"","o":[{"l":"A","t":"Direct him to the Wayward Inn."},{"l":"B","t":"Encourage him to go to a new inn run by an old friend of yours instead."}]},
    69: {"n":"A sickly looking Valrath with a long scar along his neck sits down at your table at the Sleeping Lion, uninvited. His hands are calloused and you can see the hilt of a dagger up his sleeve.\n\n\"Hm, hello. I am looking for an associate of mine. Word is that they were seen in this bar. Perhaps you know them? They would be carrying a card. A black card. Hm?\" You don't like where this is going.\n\n\"If you have seen my associate, I would be very thankful. Very thankful, hm? We have been looking for this associate for some time, and I will be finding them soon. My friendship can be lucrative. You know this, hm?\"\n\nIf they're this close, it won't be long before the Sin-Ra Syndicate discovers the holder of the Black Card, and the penalty for betrayal is death.\n\nThe holder of the Black Card decides:","o":[{"l":"A","t":"Kill the Sin-Ra Syndicate member."},{"l":"B","t":"Reveal yourself to the Sin-Ra Syndicate member."}]},
    70: {"n":"Most of the time, staying under the notice of the city's populace is the best course of action, but a little bit of information can go a long way to putting your mind at ease. You decide to take the risk of visiting a Sin-Ra gathering to gather intelligence. Sleeper cells are disconnected, so it's unlikely anyone would recognize another member on sight, and your Black Card should be all the proof necessary for admittance.\n\nYou knock at the door of a disused butcher shop in the Boiler's District. A suspicious eye appears at the peephole, and you flash your Black Card. Satisfied, the old iron door swings open to let you into a room filled with a variety of individuals from all walks of life, each agents of the Sin-Ra awaiting activation.\n\nConversation is easy, and it seems everyone's guard is down. Eventually, the topic shifts to rumors. \"I heard a cell was murdered, their Black Card stolen,\" one loose-tongued woman says. \"Sounds like a bounty will be set soon.\"","o":[{"l":"A","t":"Keep your cover and shift the topic to other matters."},{"l":"B","t":"Attempt to throw them off your trail."}]},
    71: {"n":"It's midnight. A knock on your door rouses you from sleep.\n\nYou open it to find a familiar face you'd assumed long dead: your old friend, who is working to take down the Sin-Ra Syndicate. They touch a finger to their lips, letting out a slight, \"Shh.\" You invite them inside and shut the door behind them.\n\nThey pull down a black hood and give a half-smile at the sight of you. Rather than relish the moment, though, they return to the task at hand. \"It's time—we have to strike tonight. The Grand Vizier of the Sin-Ra Syndicate is coming to Gloomhaven this very evening. We won't have another chance. Will you help me cut off the snake's head?\"","o":[{"l":"A","t":"Agree to help your friend."},{"l":"B","t":"Agree, then betray your friend to the Sin-Ra."}]},
    72: {"n":"\"Yes, well then. There is the small matter of the tontine.\" An actuary sits across the table from you, shuffling through paperwork and looking truly out of place in the Sleeping Lion's back booth. \"Your... ahem, your 'friend' in the Sin-Ra insisted that you be listed as the beneficiary. And as best as I can determine, they were the final surviving member, having outlasted all other participants in their little... club.\" He raises an eyebrow.\n\n\"You are familiar with tontines, yes? All participants pay in towards a common annuity, and the final proceeds are awarded to the surviving member. It is in my best interests to not question how the final result is made, merely to report its veracity.\"\n\nHe folds his hands. \"The tontine proceeds are 50 gold, one for each ahem... exhausted member.\" He sets a rather large leather sack on the table.","o":[{"l":"A","t":"Take the money."},{"l":"B","t":"Refuse the money."},{"l":"C","t":"Ask to establish a tontine between your current party."}]},
    73: {"n":"You decide to unwind at the Sleeping Lion, but just as you start to relax, a bear of a man crashes into your table, scattering your drinks across the floor.\n\nTowering over him is a massive Inox. \"What did you say about my horns?\" the Inox shouts.\n\nThe man stands up and brushes shards of glass from his tunic. \"I just said I mistook you for a Valrath... barely even noticed the third one. Puny, isn't it?\"\n\nThe Inox roars and charges headlong into the man, crashing through more tables in the process. At this, the entire tavern erupts into violence. After all, when someone is deep into drink, the last thing you want to do is knock that drink over.","o":[{"l":"A","t":"Join the fray!"},{"l":"B","t":"Take the opportunity to lighten some purses."},{"l":"C","t":"Do your best to stop the fighting in this respectable establishment."}]},
    74: {"n":"\"Lean in, let me show you how Orchids store memories. You tell me if it's worth a drink.\" An Orchid drags his knuckles across the tabletop in the Sleeping Lion, knocking the grime off a prominent crystal jutting out of the back of his hand. With a calm twist, he snaps it off, rubbing it back and forth between his fingertips. He draws it towards his mouth, exhaling a low, baritone note as he concentrates on the crystal. If the room wasn't dark, you wouldn't notice it, but at the edges of your vision you see an erratic motion of light swirling its way into the crystal like water circling the drain. His eyes snap open, the endowment complete. He drops the crystal on the table between you with a surprising clatter.\n\n\"Memories start ragged, like that. Ruminate on them long enough, and you'll polish your memory to perfect clarity.\" He lays a strand of small spherical pebbles on the table, strung together on a loop of string. \"It's good for reflecting. Orchids call them a 'cobble,' a set of worry-beads made from their own memories of triumphs and failures.\"\n\nHe sees your fascination. \"Tell you what. You buy the next round, and you can each have one.\"","o":[{"l":"A","t":"Each character may choose individually: \n\nOption A: Ask for a memory of perseverance.\n\nOption B: Ask for a memory of serenity."}]},
    75: {"n":"The stage in the town square is being prepared for tomorrow's event: an archery contest. The winner will need to fire an arrow through the gaps in twelve lined-up axe heads, with lesser prizes for those unable to make the impossible shot. In the decade the competition has gone on, not a single person has ever managed the feat—but the crowds show up anyway, eager to watch the attempts and enjoy the offerings of the vendors who support the festival. You're looking forward to trying your skill—and your luck—tomorrow.\n\nYou walk by after dark as the organizers install the last of the axe heads, ensuring with a long slender pole that there's a true path through. When they're finally satisfied, they throw a canvas sheet over the setup, packing in for the night. Other than your presence, the town square is now quiet for the evening.","o":[{"l":"A","t":"Take a test shot in preparation for tomorrow's competition."},{"l":"B","t":"Go home to get a good night's rest for the competition."},{"l":"C","t":"Sabotage the axe setup slightly to ensure no one will win."}]},
    76: {"n":"Harrowers are a rare sight, both outside of Gloomhaven and within. Which explains why you are a bit surprised to see three Harrowers in ornate robes step cautiously off a barge onto the dock. The dockworkers look wary, bordering on concerned.\n\n\"We have come from across the sea for the spices we require,\" one chitters at you. \"Can you direct us to the appropriate merchant? We will pay for your escort.\"\n\nA bit caught off guard, you ask about the specific kind of spice. It extends an arm, a beetle dancing across the cuff of its robe. The movement... is it trying to describe the spice through motion? After a moment, the Harrower sees it is failing to communicate and makes a series of frustrated hisses.\n\n\"It is very important, and difficult to describe. We will know when it is present.\"\n\nYou could bring them to the Sinking Market across town, which would have common spices at good prices. Or you could bring them to the New Market for high-end luxury, foreign, and exotic goods.","o":[{"l":"A","t":"Escort the Harrowers to the Sinking Market."},{"l":"B","t":"Escort the Harrowers to the New Market."},{"l":"C","t":"(Cannot be selected)"}]},
    77: {"n":"Some days you're swinging an axe against great evil, and some days you're extracting a bug infestation from a humble home in the Boiler's District. Today is unfortunately the latter.\n\nThe smoke bomb wasn't cheap, but with the insects stunned, it makes for easy cleanup. You hold your breath and work in shifts, entering the house and sweeping out the insects. When the job is done, you wind up with a decent pile of them loaded into a wooden crate. The homeowner thanks you for your time and happily pays your fee.\n\nThe bugs are starting to stir from their gas-nap, but it'll probably be another hour before they're fully awake. The question is, what to do with a box full of bugs?","o":[{"l":"A","t":"Release them."},{"l":"B","t":"Try to convince a bartender it's a sleeping Harrower and stiff it with the tab."},{"l":"C","t":"Eat them."}]},
    78: {"n":"As you return to town after your latest adventure, First Shield Harmon approaches and gestures you to the side of the road.\n\n\"I'm embarrassed to admit this,\" they begin in a low voice, \"but we could really use some help. With all the goings-on recently and very little support from the capital, the city guard is lacking in both equipment and training for the new recruits.\"\n\nIn an effort to explain, they go into altogether too much detail about deficiencies in staffing that have led to some particularly weak defenses.\n\nHarmon looks you over. \"Everyone knows you're experienced warriors. Anything you could do to help the situation would be greatly appreciated.\"","o":[{"l":"A","t":"Agree to help the city guard."},{"l":"B","t":"Decline to help and instead sell this information to the Merchants."},{"l":"C","t":"Decline to help and instead sell this information to the Sect."}]},
    79: {"n":"Nearly two thousand years ago, humans crawled out of the caves near what is now White Oak and found the Great Oak, a powerful tree that has protected the peoples of the world ever since. A bough of that original tree has been brought to Gloomhaven for a one-of-a-kind reason.\n\nA man barks at the crowd in a White Oak accent. \"Behind me is the Void: the swirling mass of black dust that consumes all who dare enter it.\" To demonstrate the Void's effects, he tosses a metal ball past the orange safety line. It disintegrates on contact, vaporized to sparkles of light.\n\n\"Today, we intend to use this sacred branch, one of the earliest objects on this continent we can definitively date, to perform a test of arcane comparative dating, and in so doing, accurately and definitively measure the origin year of this maelstrom!\"\n\nA man beside you leans in and offers a wager: \"Fifteen gold says the Great Oak's older than the Void.\"","o":[{"l":"A","t":"Refuse the bet: the original Great Oak must be older than the Void."},{"l":"B","t":"Take the bet: the Great Oak can't be that old."}]},
    80: {"n":"You're checking the bounty board in the military barracks for any new prospects when a group of new recruits filter past you, heading into a classroom. Curious, you peek inside and snatch an agenda off the table—it looks like a series of lectures on combat scheduled throughout the day.\n\nSpymaster Argeise sees you thumbing through the paperwork and approaches you. \"Mercenaries, eh? If you want, you can stay for the seminar. Might learn a thing or two.\" She leans in, \"unless you think you're too good for that. Maybe you think you could teach this class yourself?\"\n\nYou look at the other lecturers at the front of the room—they look like seasoned veterans, likely masters of their craft.\n\n\"Well?\"","o":[{"l":"A","t":"Stay for the lectures as a student."},{"l":"B","t":"Accept Argeise's challenge and give a lesson of your own."}]},
    81: {"n":"Hail, your Aesther friend, called you to her home in the Crooked Bone for an unexpected lecture. She scrawls a semi-circle of chalk on the dusty floor as she enters the second hour explaining the interconnectedness of the universe to you.\n\n\"...which means there are fifty-five known planes of this type. And each one has its own nexus in each of the other worlds. Get it?\" You do not, and much of the vocabulary is going over your head.\n\n\"So this?\" She holds up what looks like a standard pair of boots. \"This is a nexus. It's a physical link to another plane in our world. Took ages to find one. They could be anything. A ring, a green gem, a rock... anything. Anyway, this one's boots.\"\n\nShe sets them in the middle of the chalk design she's stenciled, finally getting to the decision she summoned you for. \"If we destroy it, we close the doorway to another plane. Not a big deal for an Aesther to go around, but sure would stop most unwelcome visitors in their tracks.\"","o":[{"l":"A","t":"Reducing opportunities for interplanar interlopers makes everyone safer: destroy the boots."},{"l":"B","t":"Offer to keep the boots and personally guard the nexus instead."}]},
    82: {"n":"The dock foreman hired you as protection while he breaks bad news to the gathered crowd of twenty dockworkers. \"I'm just gonna say it: I know I'm two weeks behind, but I don't have your money. Margins were tight, then we got stiffed on a job, and... there just isn't anything to pay you with!\"\n\nSeeing the angry looks on their faces, you wonder whether the fifteen gold you received up front is worth it. That's when you spot someone you recognize in the crowd: your old friend the Bruiser. They must be earning some side money working the incoming ships. A voice shouts from the back: \"What about those mercenaries? You had the money to pay them with the money you owe us?!\"\n\nThe foreman stammers out a reply: \"There's no money, just some for necessary expenses-\"\n\n\"Protecting your hide from all of us is necessary, huh? I consider my own family pretty necessary!\" The dockworkers push forward to take their money in coin or blood. This is not going well.","o":[{"l":"A","t":"Stand your ground and fight."},{"l":"B","t":"Offer the dockworkers your earnings."}]},
    83: {"n":"Anticipating an enjoyable night of drinks at the Sleeping Lion, you enter the tavern to see a familiar Savvas sitting at your usual table. The Cragheart stands and extends both its arms.\n\n\"Ah, friends! So good to see you,\" it says. \"Sit, have a drink with me!\"","o":[{"l":"A","t":"Have a drink with the Cragheart.","r":1},{"l":"B","t":"Encourage the Cragheart to move on with its life."}]},
    84: {"n":"Food has just arrived at your table when a familiar face walks into the Sleeping Lion. It's none other than your old friend the Cragheart, albeit with a handsome-looking cape and boots that show hardworking wear.\n\nYour old friend doesn't notice you at your usual table, and it takes some effort to get its attention. It squints its eyes at you, processing your presence for a moment before the spark of recognition hits.\n\n\"Friends! I didn't see you there. It has been too long!\" The Cragheart greets each of you with a confident smile. Over the next few hours, you share stories of your own adventures, winding down the evening as you used to. You learn that the Cragheart has traveled extensively in its retirement—even further than your travels together. Eventually, though, the sun starts streaming through the tavern's curtains. There's only time for a few final words.","o":[{"l":"A","t":"Thank the Cragheart for its time in your party."},{"l":"B","t":"Ask the Cragheart to regale you with one more story from its time abroad."}]},
    85: {"n":"You're just settling into bed when the door to your room is blown clear off the hinges. You brush the splinters off your clothes and watch in horror as a rift opens where your doorway should be, gushing waves of brackish water into your room. Amidst the torrent, out flies a scepter with enough force to embed itself into the opposite wall. Wait, is that—\n\nBlood, rubble, and terrifying eye stalks flash into view through the fissure. Then a familiar hand springs out: it's the Soultether's! It reaches frantically for any kind of purchase, but before they can pull themselves through to the safety of the room, a tremendous claw slices through their torso. The Soultether hollers out in pain, then locks eyes with you. \"DESTROY THE SCEPTER!\" they scream as the rift snaps back shut. The Soultether's severed hand remains on your side of the portal.\n\nYou feel their connection to you shatter. The Soultether is dead.","o":[{"l":"A","t":"Respect the Soultether's dying wish and destroy their scepter."},{"l":"B","t":"Something this powerful should be wielded for good, no matter the cost. Keep the scepter intact."}]},
    86: {"n":"It's been some time since you last saw your friend the Mindthief, and you take a trip down to the Old Docks to the forwarding address they left behind. A knock on the door reveals an unconcerned-looking Vermling that introduces himself as Two-Tailed Taz.\n\n\"Oh, that was your friend? Passed away. Poof. Gone.\" The Vermling makes a snapping noise with his jaw to emphasize the point. Still somewhat shocked, you press for details.\n\n\"Don't drink the water, not since Roland. Don't eat the rats, either. Same reason. Last of their litter. Very old. Old. Old! Dead now.\" Lost in thought, the Vermling rubs his teeth together, producing a sharp squeaking sound. Suddenly, he darts back inside the home and drags out a ratty-looking garment you recognize. \"Oh, they left this for you. Right, right. All they had, sorry it wasn't more.\" With the message relayed, he nods to himself.\n\nYou look at the flea-bitten shawl your friend used to wear. It feels small in your hands.\n\nGain Flea-Bitten Shawl (Item 137)","o":[{"l":"A","t":"Say a few words for your fallen friend."},{"l":"B","t":"Doubt the Vermling's story."}]},
    87: {"n":"\"You look surprised to see me.\" Your old friend the Mindthief starts walking beside you through the Sinking Market. \"Not dead yet, don't worry. And if you hear otherwise from Two-Tailed Taz, know he's a liar.\"\n\nYour old friend seems right at home walking and talking with you, and for a moment, it feels like they never left.\n\n\"There are good Vermlings and bad Vermlings. Some bad Vermlings are killing villagers in the Hook Coast.\" The Vermling stops, looking you squarely in the eye. \"Bad Vermlings are bad for all Vermlings. My adventuring days are over, but someone needs to help the villagers build defenses and learn to take care of themselves.\"\n\nIt's quickly becoming clear the Mindthief thinks that someone is you.","o":[{"l":"A","t":"Agree to help the villagers of the Hook Coast."},{"l":"B","t":"Friend or not, demand payment for the trouble."}]},
    88: {"n":"Slumped outside the Sleeping Lion, you find your old friend the Nightshroud. Surprised to see them—for several reasons—you ask what's the matter.\n\n\"Normally, I am not-here, but here. Lately, I've been not-here, and not-here.\" The words spill out, not entirely sensical. \"When my mind and my body are both present, I understand things. When they are out of sync, it is more difficult. To think.\"\n\nThe Nightshroud stands, a bit uneasy on their own legs. With a concerned look down to their hands they vanish, reappearing behind you. They continue, having not noticed the change.\n\n\"Am I thread? I fear I am unwound.\"","o":[{"l":"A","t":"Encourage the Nightshroud to anchor itself to this plane."},{"l":"B","t":"Encourage the Nightshroud to let itself drift to other planes."}]},
    89: {"n":"\"Stay back, it's likely to jump to others!\" A man clutches at his stomach, boils spreading over his body. He takes a few faltering steps before dropping to the ground, dead. The town guard is holding back a growing crowd, fearful themselves to approach.\n\nA citizen whispers something in the ear of a guard, pointing in your direction. He steps up to you with a concerned look. \"I hear you used to travel with a Harrower. One who would kill like this. That true?\"\n\nYou stammer a reply, but he's heard enough. \"I hear your friend was the last to talk to that man. None of us are going near that—you're going to clean this up yourselves. Standard wage for the body removal, but this is on you.\"\n\nAnother lingering gift from the Plagueherald.","o":[{"l":"A","t":"Protect yourself as best you can and remove the body."},{"l":"B","t":"This isn't your mess—refuse to help the guards."}]},
    90: {"n":"The rumors are true: a pox is spreading through Gloomhaven, affecting anyone foolish to wander the streets after dark.\n\nYou're stepping out of the Sleeping Lion when you see it: a Harrower in a ragged cloak holding a man down in an alley as its swarm spreads out and consumes him. The man's screams are muffled as the insects skitter down his throat, depositing their sticky-sweet infection across his insides.\n\nKilling monsters and demons is one thing, but the Plagueherald shouldn't be preying on people in the city. You approach it, intent on stopping its attack.","o":[{"l":"A","t":"Calmly explain the situation to the Plagueherald, imploring it to move on."},{"l":"B","t":"Enough is enough—you'll need to exterminate your old friend."}]},
    91: {"n":"You catch sight of your old mercenary companion, the Quartermaster, pulling a wagon through town. Piled high are weapons, armor, and all manner of trinkets, all held down with series of expertly-tied ropes. They're delighted to see you.\n\n\"Hello friends! Just finished acquiring these in the Sinking Market. Lots of good deals on second-hand goods today. With a bit of repair and polish, I'll be able to sell them good as new. Probably priced a bit higher than new, actually. Not much is out there... People are getting concerned with the stability of the city, thanks to all the politics. Hoarding.\" They wipe the sweat from their brow, and are just about to push on when they turn back to you.\n\n\"Say, you wouldn't have anything you're not using anymore, would you? I'd be happy to buy it from you, full price.\"","o":[{"l":"A","t":"Sell the Quartermaster some of your equipment at face value."},{"l":"B","t":"Ask to purchase some of the used equipment before it gets resold."}]},
    92: {"n":"Your old friend the Sawbones sits down at your usual table at the Sleeping Lion and immediately launches into recounting recent adventures.\n\n\"I miss working with you all, of course. I felt like I always had steady work and steady income, which was nice. I had a bit of a rough patch a few weeks ago, but it was fine: my friend Bonecruncher lent me some money.\"\n\nOh no. Bonecruncher, the notorious loan shark? The Sawbones sees the look on your faces and meets it with an eye roll. \"Don't worry about it, it's not some big racket like everyone makes it out to be. I don't even have to pay it all back at once—just a little bit of interest every now and then. He explained everything, I'll have it paid back in no time at all.\"\n\nBefore you can object, the Sawbones reaches into his satchel. \"Besides, I got you all some nice gifts, since I've got more room in my finances now!\"","o":[{"l":"A","t":"Accept a generous gift from your old friend."},{"l":"B","t":"Pay off the Sawbones' debt with Bonecruncher immediately."}]},
    93: {"n":"Your old friend the Sawbones slides into your usual table at the Sleeping Lion, exhausted from a long day helping heal the unfortunate at the Temple of the Great Oak.\n\n\"I got to use the old saw today, so that was nice.\" The Sawbones trails off, clearly thinking about better times. Finally, they snap back to it, asking you the question they've been considering for some time.\n\n\"What would you think if I... stopped being a healer. I've accomplished my dreams, but now I just feel... stuck, I guess.\" The Sawbones refuses to make eye contact, staring down at the bar top. \"I know I'm helping at the Temple, and they really need the skills I have. But I just can't help wanting something more.\"","o":[{"l":"A","t":"Remind the Sawbones how important their work is.","r":1},{"l":"B","t":"Encourage the Sawbones to pursue a new dream."}]},
    94: {"n":"It's performance night at the Brown Door, a seedy bar across town. You're in attendance to root on your old friend the Soothsinger, who is competing in this evening's battle of the bands. A drummer makes a lot of noise, singers croon at the crowd, and melodies grace the stage. Each are met with various levels of enthusiasm from the audience, and it's quickly apparent who the top contenders are.\n\nWaiting to go on stage next is Basseloff, a pretentious Quatryl bard who styles himself \"the greatest in the world.\" You're standing nearby as he tunes his lute with meticulous precision, plucking each string and warbling to the frequency. It is obnoxious, and you can't help but feel sorry for the singer onstage who must be able to hear it.\n\nAs the prior act is drawing to a close, a group of fans see Basseloff and swarm, much to his apparent delight. In the chaos, Basseloff sets down his lute, just within arms-reach of you.","o":[{"l":"A","t":"Quickly untune the lute."},{"l":"B","t":"The Soothsinger is good enough to win the contest without foul play."}]},
    95: {"n":"In the very early morning, you are awakened by a knock at your door. Standing behind it is a giant, dirty Inox wearing a leather apron.\n\n\"Oh, uh... ahem,\" he coughs. \"Sorry, I was looking for an Orchid friend of mine. They used to help me on occasion with my forge. I thought this was the right room. Sorry, it's just their control over fire was astounding, and I paid them well for assistance in completing some of my specialty work. Just can't get the fires stoked the same way without a bit of that, er...\" he waves his hands in a fashion similar to what your old friend the Spellweaver would do, \"...that.\"\n\nHe scratches his head. \"Guess that's not an option any more. You wouldn't happen to know where I could find someone that could help me in their absence, would you?\"","o":[{"l":"A","t":"Offer to help the blacksmith yourself."},{"l":"B","t":"Offer to summon a demon to stoke the flames."}]},
    96: {"n":"You've arrived at a cordoned-off area in the Temple of the Great Oak, beside the tree itself. In shining, polished armor before you is your old companion the Sunkeeper. \"Thank you for coming. There is a tear in the Oak, and I fear it is beyond me to repair on my own.\"\n\nShielded from public view by a black curtain, a waist-high gash runs up the tree, revealing past the bark. Inside, the wood looks warm, vulnerable but comforting. You step closer and almost feel you sense a slow heartbeat—\n\nThe Sunkeeper puts a hand on your shoulder. \"Be not enticed, we have work at hand. Normally, this is a secret task for several Sunkeepers working in concert, but all save myself are away on pressing business. The injury must be mended—either through care, or through cauterization. Which would be most comfortable to your skills?\"","o":[{"l":"A","t":"Use magic to attempt to repair the Great Oak's injury."},{"l":"B","t":"Cauterize the damaged portion of the tree."}]},
    97: {"n":"They're two hours late, and you're starting to get bored. You're being paid a healthy sum to facilitate the sale of whatever is in these mystery crates. Your curiosity is starting to outweigh your patience.\n\nJust then, you hear a voice outside the warehouse. \"You go in first, I'll direct the reinforcements when they arrive. Ready? Go.\" A split-second later, the door is blown off its hinges with the swing of a massive warhammer. When the dust settles, you see your old friend the Sunkeeper, who looks as surprised as you are.\n\n\"Wait—you're the ones selling stolen oak?\" The Sunkeeper pries open one of the crates to show you: stacked in neat piles inside are boughs of the Great Oak. The contraband supposedly has protective powers, but the Keepers make sure it never falls into the wrong hands. This must've been sawn off illegally for sale on the black market.\n\n\"What were you thinking? The town guard will be here any minute with reinforcements!\"","o":[{"l":"A","t":"Stick around and explain you were just being paid to sell the crates."},{"l":"B","t":"Ask the Sunkeeper to let you go before the town guard arrives."},{"l":"C","t":"Ask the Sunkeeper to let you go, with the crates."}]},
    98: {"n":"You return to your room to find your old friend the Tinkerer on their hands and knees, grabbing at something under the floorboards. A leather backpack beside them is overflowing with polished brass mechanical limbs. \"Drat, I could've sworn... There you are—oh, hello!\" They look surprised to see you in your own quarters. \"Oh, right. I realized I left some equipment here from back when we were adventuring together. I've been working on some top secret inventions. I needed this—\" your old friend holds up a wooden handle with metal sockets on it.\n\n\"Spider-piston extender. Huge pain working on spider bots without one... Not, uh, not that I'm making spider bots.\" They look like they've already said too much.\n\n\"Anyway, I should probably let you get back to... Uhm. Unless you'd be willing to do some field testing on one of my creations?\" They look incredibly hopeful.","o":[{"l":"A","t":"Agree to test a heal-spraying spider bot."},{"l":"B","t":"Agree to test an aggressive, poison-spewing spider bot."}]},
    99: {"n":"Your old friend the Tinkerer barges into your room holding a confusing metal box. \"You're here! A Quatryl asked me to build a time-dilation machine. Should work perfectly. But if it doesn't, we need to hit the red button, green button twice, blue, then red three more times. Everyone ready? Great!\"\n\nWithout waiting, the Tinkerer throws a lever. Time slows to the point of irritation—even attempting to refocus your vision is boredom-inducing. And then you see the sparks, and fire... you need to hit the sequence of buttons together and shut it down.\n\nSolo players: read section 81.2 instead of the rest of this event.\n\nEach character secretly selects C+2 of their ability cards. Then, without communicating with each other in any way, a character starts a pile of face-up cards in the center of the table by playing their lowest initiative card. Characters must put down all of their cards in increasing order in the center of the table on an open stack, one after another. You may not play a card of an initiative lower than the top card of the pile. There are no turns, and characters may play cards as they see fit. When you can no longer play additional cards, flip this card.","o":[{"l":"A","t":"Count the number of cards that were unable to be played."}]},
    100: {"n":"You're greeted with a gust of cold air: crawling through the windowsill is your old friend the Wildfury. They tumble unceremoniously to the floor, brushing some dirt out of their fur.\n\n\"Sorry, I think someone changed the lock. I would've knocked, but... I didn't.\" They sit on the edge of the bed that used to be theirs before retirement, seemingly unaware of just how filthy the pelts they're covered in are. The Wildfury produces a piece of jerky, chewing as they speak. \"Loggers disturbed the spirits of the wilds. I need your help to calm them, or destroy them.\"\n\nIt's not clear whether your old friend is referring to the spirits or the loggers. They toss the end bit of the jerky onto the floor without finishing it, then raise an eyebrow at your concerned reaction. \"What? Vermlings never eat the last bite of food. Bad luck. This way you always know there's more somewhere.\"\n\nThey hop off the bed. \"We remove the blight or we suffer the consequences. So? Will you help me?\"","o":[{"l":"A","t":"Help your old friend calm the forest."},{"l":"B","t":"The Wildfury will have to do this alone, you draw the line at killing loggers."}]},
  },
  road: {
    1: {"n":"A collection of travelers surround a campfire up ahead and beckons you to join them. They are sharing stories as the sunsets for the evening and seem friendly enough.\n\nA Valrath passes a flagon of sweet meekseed wine around the circle as he tells of his adventures along the southern coast. It's an incredible mix of adventure, fun, and bad fortune with impossible odds, and it certainly makes for a good campfire story. The story winds down and the assembled group looks to you to contribute a tale.","o":[{"l":"A","t":"Tell a scary story."},{"l":"B","t":"Make up an adventure and claim it's true."}]},
    2: {"n":"The scraping snap of claws warns you of what's coming before you see it: there's a Lurker up ahead. You have no desire to tangle with the massive crab creatures, and with any luck, it hasn't heard you yet.\n\nWhen you finally lay eyes on it, you see what's causing the ruckus: a full-sized Lurker has gotten itself caught in a bear trap by the river bank. It is frantically snapping its claws at the metal but can't get a decent angle to detach the jaws. Judging by how slowly it's moving, it's been struggling for some time.\n\nWhen it spots you, it rears up, brandishing its claws in your direction. With one leg trapped, though, this one looks far from the fearsome tyrants they normally are on the battlefield-instead, this one looks like easy prey.","o":[{"l":"A","t":"Take down the Lurker before it frees itself."},{"l":"B","t":"Assist the Lurker in removing the trap."}]},
    3: {"n":"\"Don't come any closer! On penalty of death! Your death! From unluckiness!\" Standing up the road is a stout Quatryl with various cushions and pillows strapped to his body. The configuration seems to make it difficult for him to breathe. \"I'm warning you! I am dangerously unlucky! Approach at your own risk!\" As if to emphasize the point, he promptly falls over, smashing his nose into the ground.\n\nA trickle of blood runs down his face. \"Oh no, it's… yeah, that's blood.\" At the sight of it he gives a half-stumble, then promptly passes out in the road.\n\nYou prop him up to be comfortable, then splash cool water across his face. He wakes up with a start. \"No! You're too near me! You'll get the curse! I'm the unluckiest Quatryl that ever lived, you need to stay back!\"","o":[{"l":"A","t":"Assure him you're here to help however you can."},{"l":"B","t":"Kill the Quatryl to release him from his accursed fate."}]},
    4: {"n":"By the side of the road, you find a carriage parked, its owner wiping sweat from his brow. An old Vermling gives a toothless grin in your direction, waving you over to the makeshift shop he's set up.\n\n\"Gloomhaven Council Law 119.5, important one, you heard of it? Sets the patrol boundary for the town guard just over that ridge there. This patch of road is outside their jurisdiction. Lets me keep the prices low, eh? Lots to see, priced to move. Wheels to move, too,\" He kicks the side of his carriage, \"just in case that law ever changes. Heh heh.\" He chuckles to himself as he lays thick carpets by the side of the road, spreading out his wares—several unusual artifacts catch your eye.\n\n\"I'm just doing my part to make sure these hard-to-find items stay in circulation, eh? No touching! Browse with your eyes, not your hands.\"","o":[{"l":"A","t":"Browse the available wares.","r":1},{"l":"B","t":"Assure him you're not concerned with where his items come from.","r":1}]},
    5: {"n":"You're a few steps outside of Gloomhaven's walls when a man with a pair of horses and a wagon stops you. He flashes a broad, inviting smile.\n\n\"Care for a ride to your destination? Anywhere you want to go, just five gold.\"","o":[{"l":"A","t":"Take the man up on his offer."},{"l":"B","t":"Take your chances on the road."}]},
    6: {"n":"You stop to allow a procession of Inox to pass. They're dressed in ceremonial garb and led by a trio of Savvas chanting a solemn dirge and thumping their chests in time to the group's footfalls. At the center of the group, you spot a young Inox woman sobbing behind a white veil, her hands bound with silk rope. No one appears willing to comfort her.\n\nThe display is unusual enough that you peel off an Inox at the back of the congregation to ask what's going on.\n\n\"We're heading to the Rupture.\" He gestures to black smoke rising from a far-off peak. \"The beast awakens and demands a sacrifice. We must sate its anger! The mountain is aflame!\"\n\nThe woman falls to her knees, wailing in terror. The Savvas stop their chanting to order the group into action, and two men in flowing robes pick her off the ground. When she refuses to walk, they drag her as best they can, still sobbing.","o":[{"l":"A","t":"Let the procession pass."},{"l":"B","t":"Offer yourself as a sacrifice in her stead."},{"l":"C","t":"Kill the convoy and free the woman."}]},
    7: {"n":"A set of abandoned fishing poles rests beside a feeder stream. The poles look like they were hastily discarded in the tall grass, and a bucket of worms writhe in a wicker basket beside them. With your stomach rumbling, you grab a pole and make use of your good fortune.","o":[{"l":"A","t":"Each character may cast their line up to five times, and may stop at any time. Characters may cast in any order of their choosing, but should not shuffle between casts. Each time a character casts their line, draw a card from their attack modifier deck. Evaluate only the numerical value of the card, ignoring any additional effects. If there is no numerical value, set it aside and draw again."}],"r":1},
    8: {"n":"That's strange. There's something in the road here… ah, it's a corpse. A freshly-gnawed corpse. But it's beside a sturdy-looking mace that would look great on your hip—\n\nPfft-TUFF!\n\nA sharp barb lodges itself into your leg, and the idyllic hillside takes a turn for drowsy. You try to keep your bearings as a group of Vermlings rappel down from the trees and launch their attack, blow darts and spears in tow.\n\nAs you fight the sedative effects of the poison and struggle to stay awake, you recall that Vermlings have been known to eat the dead. It is not a comforting thought as your eyelids get heavy.","o":[{"l":"A","t":"Defend yourself through the sleepiness."},{"l":"B","t":"Grab the heavy mace and try to make a run for it."}]},
    9: {"n":"Not far outside Gloomhaven, you look up to see a large bird flying overhead. Something is odd, though. Its movements are jerky, and there is smoke billowing out of it. Then you see it plunge into a sudden nose-dive and crash to the ground off in the east.\n\nYou rush to the scene and find a limping, soot-covered Quatryl kicking a giant winged contraption made of leather and metal.\n\n\"Curse this wretched thing!\" He yells in frustration. \"I thought I'd worked it out, and then I suddenly lost pressure in the piston chamber!\"\n\nHe looks over at you. \"You there! Wonderful timing! Help me get this thing back into the air. There is no time to waste—the world needs my invention!\"","o":[{"l":"A","t":"Examine the machinery yourself to determine the cause of the malfunction."},{"l":"B","t":"Follow the inventor's directions and perform the repairs."}]},
    10: {"n":"The distant whine of machinery draws you to a curious contraption: impossibly embedded into the mountainside is a metal doorway and several blinking crystals. A plaque at the top reads \"Emergency Temporal Stash.\"","o":[{"l":"A","t":"The crystals have a sheen unlike any you've seen. Touch one."},{"l":"B","t":"Press the button in the center of the console."}]},
    11: {"n":"You are feeling a tad hungry as you walk down the road. You are considering stopping for a meal when you come across a thicket of bushes covered in red berries.\n\nThe berries look delicious, but you hesitate. They could be poisonous.","o":[{"l":"A","t":"Eat the berries.","r":1},{"l":"B","t":"Pass by the berries and just eat your normal rations.","r":1}]},
    12: {"n":"As you walk down a dirt path, you see a hard-looking mercenary sitting in a patch of grass. He nods as you pass.\n\n\"Hey, friends,\" he says. There is something off about his tone, though.\n\n\"I don't suppose one of you might be willing to part with a stamina potion, would you? I'm headed toward Gloomhaven, but I've just come from such a long way, and I'm not feeling too good about the stretch I have left.\"\n\nWith the clank of his sword sheath against his armor and a loud groan, the man stands up. \"I'll pay you well for it.\"","o":[{"l":"A","t":"Sell the man a stamina potion."},{"l":"B","t":"Politely decline and move quickly on your way."}]},
    13: {"n":"After a heavy rain, the roads are dark streaks of slushy dirt. A group of exhausted attendants are pitifully attempting to free a wagon caught in knee-deep mud. The side of the wagon is emblazoned with \"The Great Dallum—the Seer Who Knows.\"\n\nWith a moment of help you manage to clear it out of the rut. As soon as it's free, the side door opens and out steps a man with a charisma unlike any you've seen. Long, sandy-brown hair cascades down his shoulders, and his watery blue eyes gently meet your gaze. With a warm smile, he addresses you by name, then gives a friendly chuckle at your surprise.\n\n\"Thank you for your help—I know not how long we would have been trapped here were it not for you.\" He clasps your hands in his own, and you feel the earnest callouses of a man who is no stranger to hard work. \"I can see eddies flowing through us, and I use my gifts to help others. We were on our way to Gloomhaven when the rains caught us. Before we continue, can I thank you by lending my sight to your needs?\"","o":[{"l":"A","t":"Ask that he looks into the future of Gloomhaven."},{"l":"B","t":"Ask that he looks into the future of your party."}]},
    14: {"n":"It's late afternoon when your progress is stopped by a thicket of brambles that have overtaken the road. It's hard work clearing the path, and during a break, you spot a small outpost with a good vantage point to the horizon. It's a little ways off the road, and even from a distance you can see it is being strangled by the same brambles that have made your journey frustrating so far.\n\nThere are a few hours before nightfall: diverting and clearing a path to the outpost would be hard labor for the rest of the afternoon, but having a safe place to rest would be welcome. If you do a good job, you may even be able to use it in the future. Is it worth the effort today?","o":[{"l":"A","t":"Spend the rest of the day clearing brambles to get to the outpost."},{"l":"B","t":"Clear only what's needed to continue to your destination.","r":1}]},
    15: {"n":"The distant sound of popping washes over the landscape, increasing in intensity as you approach. Before you is a field of trees bearing amberhollow, a brittle, hollow fruit that is entirely comprised of its shell with nothing but a bit of sweet liquid and an intense vacuum at its center. They're a novel treat enjoyed for the explosive cracking sound they make when opened. The larger the fruit, the larger the vacuum and the harder they are to transport, leaving a very short (and sometimes dangerous) window for picking.\n\nThis orchard of amberhollow trees is certainly ripe, and all signs would point to it being an excellent harvest. Unfortunately, a pair of flame demons are wreaking havoc up and down the rows, lighting the heavy branches aflame, and the fire has spread to the only building on the property. The whole crop will be nothing but ash in minutes, and the burned corpse of the farmer at your feet probably won't be enjoying the fruits of his labor, either.","o":[{"l":"A","t":"Confront the flame demons."},{"l":"B","t":"Gather some fruits from the trees and make a quick exit."},{"l":"C","t":"Push past the demons and raid the farmer's former home before it burns up."}]},
    16: {"n":"You're trudging along by the confidence of moonlight when an unfortunate misstep sends you tumbling down into a ditch. To your horror, your fall is stopped by slamming into an old Orchid meditating out of sight from the road. An Orchid sustains itself merely from contact with the ground and exposure to the sun, so it's not uncommon for one to detach from society for days or even months at a time. With a yawn she rouses herself, taking stock of the night air.\n\n\"Ho there! Been napping for a spell, what year is it?\" The year? You tell her it's 1958 LO. \"That'll make it just shy of two hundred years between measurements, then. And on a full moon, too! Excellent timing.\" She drags a heavy blanket off an apparatus next to her which reveals the largest telescope you've ever seen.\n\n\"Drat! The optic's cracked. Must've had a snap frost some year. Suppose I'll have to journey to the nearest town for parts. Can you point me towards Wayward? I assume the Sharp 'n' Sturdy still stands, even after all these years.\"","o":[{"l":"A","t":"Attempt to repair the optics yourself."},{"l":"B","t":"Explain that Gloomhaven is much closer than Wayward."}]},
    17: {"n":"A human boy is pacing back and forth in front of the mouth of a cave. When he sees you, the teen starts in your direction, then thinks better of it, turning back to his pacing. You approach and ask him what's the matter. He wrings his hands as he explains.\n\n\"It's… I lost a bet with some older kids. I'm supposed to go into this cave to prove I'm not a coward. It's just… those friends were supposed to meet me here to watch.\" He starts speaking at his shoes, unwilling to make eye contact with you. \"I… I think they got here first and they're waiting in there to scare me. But if I don't go in, then they'll know I really am a coward. I don't know what to do.\"","o":[{"l":"A","t":"Convince him to face his fear and go into the cave."},{"l":"B","t":"Explain he has nothing to prove."}]},
    18: {"n":"A few minutes outside of Gloomhaven, a well-fed raven is pacing in the middle of the road. It cocks its head as you approach, seemingly unafraid and used to interacting with people. When you get close enough, it hops forward, presenting its left leg. A small scrap of parchment is tied to it with a black ribbon.\n\nThe note is sealed with black wax and the insignia of a serpent wrapped around a dagger. You unfurl the note and read:\n\n\"It has been nearly a month since your last check-in. Advise immediately whether you have installed the puppet stewards as agreed.\"\n\nThe raven presents its other leg, waiting for a reply to be tied on.","o":[{"l":"A","t":"Report that the preparations are on track."},{"l":"B","t":"Report that this went to the wrong individual."},{"l":"C","t":"Report back with a surprise (requires \"Escaping the Sin-Ra\" Personal Quest)."}]},
    19: {"n":"You're a long distance from Gloomhaven when an exhausted Valrath approaches on foot from the other direction, leading an equally spent hristek by its reins.\n\n\"Hello, travelers,\" the Valrath says. \"I'm embarrassed to ask, but I was on a mission from the city, and I seem to have ridden too far, too fast. Both my mount and I are tired and out of supplies—would you allow us to rest with you this evening?\"\n\nYou agree, and when you finally stop to make camp, the grateful traveler opens his saddlebag to share his remaining supplies. As he does, a wooden lockbox tumbles out onto the ground. He quickly scoops it up, but not before you catch sight of the symbol carved into its lid: a trident rising through a spiral.\n\n\"Silly me,\" he smiles as he stuffs the box back into his bag and produces a few paltry pieces of cured meat. \"Now, friends, let's sit around the fire and eat, yes?\"","o":[{"l":"A","t":"Sneak over and open the lockbox while he's distracted with dinner."},{"l":"B","t":"Let his secrets lie—ask him about his mission instead."}]},
    20: {"n":"Headed in the opposite direction, back towards Gloomhaven, are a pair of guards escorting four men clasped in manacles. As you pass them, one of the prisoners forces the gag out of his mouth, shouting at you for assistance: \"Help us, please! We're political prisoners!\"\n\nOne of the guards gives him a swift jab in the stomach and replaces the gag. \"Sure, political prisoners. More like these four are treasonous scum. We're taking them back to the Ghost Fortress for processing.\" The guard yanks the chain-gang forward, snarling at the one who spoke up. \"Enjoy the rest of your walk, it's the last gulps of fresh air you'll have before they seal you away for good.\"\n\nThe guards give a laugh, turning to you for your reaction. That's all it takes for the four men to make a run for it, stumbling up the embankment towards the cover of trees.\n\nIt takes a moment before the guards realize what's happening, and even then, all they manage is an impotent cry of, \"Stop them!\"","o":[{"l":"A","t":"Assist the guards in recapturing their charges."},{"l":"B","t":"Let the prisoners escape."}]},
    21: {"n":"Just outside the range where Gloomhaven could reasonably extend its influence, you find a clearing with a friendly-looking Quatryl in formal clothes beckoning you over. The Quatryl, bursting with excitement, gives a sweep of his arm to introduce you to the device beside him.\n\n\"Welcome to the Slot Sanctum, a traveling slot machine that can make your wildest dreams come true with a spin of the dial!\" He gestures to the machine with flair. \"Win fabulous prizes!\" He calls your attention to the small table of stacked coins, exotic furs, and mystery boxes wrapped in red ribbon.\n\nGiving it a spin couldn't hurt.","o":[{"l":"A","t":"Play the slot machine legitimately.","r":1},{"l":"B","t":"Distract the Quatryl operator to gain an advantage."}]},
    22: {"n":"Every single time you leave the walls of Gloomhaven, you follow the road to the right at the copse of trees with the arrow-shaped rock, the beaten path, the one that has faithfully led you to adventure. It is a reliable road, and to be frank, you've never heard anyone who bothered taking the short footpath that leads into the grass to the left.\n\nYou asked around the Sleeping Lion one drunken night and you were laughed off: everyone knows to take the path to the right when leaving the city. It's just common knowledge. But when you pressed, no one could give an adequate reason why.\n\nPerhaps today is the day. The day you will finally take control of your life and step outside the comforts of familiarity. Today is the day to go left at the fork.","o":[{"l":"A","t":"Go left."},{"l":"B","t":"Go right.","r":1}]},
    23: {"n":"An old man sits by the side of the road. \"I suppose you're here to kill me, then?\" You make clear you have no idea who he is, and he nods solemnly.\n\n\"I met an Aesther once. She told me she could see the infinite. So I asked her to tell me my future... dumbest mistake I ever made.\" He starts unbuttoning his coat. \"She said she could tell me just two things: first, that a group looking just like you would kill me on this day, on this road. And second, that if I ever spoke to my son again, even a single word in a letter, he'd die the very next day.\"\n\n\"I left right then. Fifty years I didn't talk to the boy I loved. He passed just last year, natural causes. Lived a good life, but I couldn't tell him why his dad left. Couldn't tell him I loved him one last time.\" There's a catch in the old man's throat. \"So either I lived a life of needless sorrow, or you're going to kill me today and prove that witch right.\" He folds coat neatly on the ground beside him, ready for his destiny.","o":[{"l":"A","t":"Kill the old man to prove the prophecy true."},{"l":"B","t":"Spare his life, and crush his heart."}]},
    24: {"n":"You've taken a \"shortcut\" through the sewers to get outside the city wall, but a wrong turn leaves you lost. The only way forward is through a wall of pipes. You pry one off, but a burst of water pressure knocks you to the ground and seals the door. The water is rapidly rising! There's no time to talk. In order to escape, you need to select the correct valve to turn off the flow, and you need to do it fast.","o":[{"l":"A","t":"Set this card on the table. Players may no longer communicate in any way. Once you have selected which valve to close, raise your hand. When all players' hands are raised, each player simultaneously says their chosen valve's letter aloud. Then, flip this card."}]},
    25: {"n":"A well-manicured patch of grass surrounds the imposing trunk of a massive amberhollow tree. Its fruits are delicious but fragile—the brittle shell implodes with a satisfying pop to reveal sweet liquid at the center. They last just a few days after picking.\n\nA skeleton is resting peacefully under the shade of the tree's wide leaves. The left side of its face is caved in, although you can't tell if it was before or after death. Curiously, someone has dressed it in a pair of overalls as though it were a macabre scarecrow. A bucket sits in the skeleton's lap, a few gold coins at the bottom.","o":[{"l":"A","t":"Pick some amberhollow for a snack."},{"l":"B","t":"Pay for the privilege of picking some amberhollow."},{"l":"C","t":"Take the coins from the bucket."}],"r":1},
    26: {"n":"A leisurely walk to your destination is interrupted when you stumble over a tree root sticking up from the forest floor. Upon closer inspection, you find that it is, in reality, a leg. A leg that is attached to a rather dead Savvas, half-buried under a bed of fallen leaves.\n\nYou exhume the body with a nearby stick—it's a finely dressed Savvas with the bronze insignia of the Merchant's Guild on its lapel. This looks to be a high-ranking guild member who met a very unfortunate end at the hands of a wild animal.","o":[{"l":"A","t":"Gather the valuables and bury the body."},{"l":"B","t":"Carry the body with you on your adventure, then return it intact to the Merchant's Guild."}]},
    27: {"n":"A discarded town guard tunic and belt on the side of the road catch your attention as you pass. You look around and see a guardsman squatting in the bushes a small distance away.\n\n\"Oh, hello!\" he says. \"Good timing, actually. You wouldn't mind grabbing me some leaves, would you? There's quite a mess over here.\"","o":[{"l":"A","t":"Bring the man some leaves."},{"l":"B","t":"Grab his stuff and run off while he's indisposed."}]},
    28: {"n":"POP!\n\nWith a laugh, you watch the red-mohawked leader of the Four Crosses, a rival mercenary group, throw a ripe amberhollow fruit into the air, where his companion bats it with the blunt side of his sword. It results in a loud bang of sugary liquid and laughter from his coarse companions. They're systematically pillaging the contents of a wagon.\n\nA farmer and his wife plead with them amidst the laughter. \"Surely you needn't destroy them needlessly, they're fragile—have pity!\"\n\nThe leader chuckles as he hops into the wagon and starts stomping the fruits underfoot. \"Hey, you said we could have half your harvest if we protected you on the journey to White Oak. We'll use our half as we please!\" The rest of the Four Crosses erupt in gruff chortles.","o":[{"l":"A","t":"The Four Crosses give mercenaries a bad name. Stand up to them."},{"l":"B","t":"It's the Four Crosses' fruit, per the deal. Side with the mercenaries."}]},
    29: {"n":"You are feeling a tad hungry as you walk down the road. You are considering stopping for a meal when you come across a thicket of bushes covered in green berries.\n\nThe berries look delicious, but you hesitate. They could be poisonous.","o":[{"l":"A","t":"Eat the berries."},{"l":"B","t":"Pass by the berries and just eat your normal rations.","r":1}]},
    30: {"n":"\"Lantern Beetles, that's what I call them, on account of I put them in these little lanterns. Handheld, lots of uses, really.\" You're chatting with an Orchid farmer as you pass by the buzzing boxes he's tending. \"They've got a connection to the ether that surrounds us like no other creature I've ever seen—they can feel it, live off it like it's food right to their tummies. You feed them that, it changes things.\n\n\"I've been farming them for going right on forty years now. There's always buyers. People who want to rid their home of dark energy love the Brilliant Beetles. I know a few folks who keep an Ignition Beetle in their kitchens to spark the stove, almost like a pet.\" He lights a candle inside one of the boxes and the beetles flock to it, brushing their mandibles against it as though they were drinking the smoke.\n\n\"Most sales probably go to the Harrowers, though—it's like a fancy drug to them. I mean, just look at these beauties. Is it any surprise they'd want to have one as a part of them?\"","o":[{"l":"A","t":"Flip this card."}]},
    31: {"n":"It's late afternoon when the road you're following cuts through a thicket of tangled brambles. You recall putting in significant effort to clear this path some time ago, and while the vegetation is doing its best to retake the trail, it's a simple matter to avoid the worst of the thorns.\n\nThe trail you blazed up to the abandoned outpost remains clear, and a good night's rest with a real roof over your head could do you some good. Given the decent condition of the road, though, you could make good time to your destination by continuing on your way.","o":[{"l":"A","t":"Make camp in the abandoned outpost for the night."},{"l":"B","t":"Take advantage of the open trail and continue on your way."}]},
    32: {"n":"Out of the sky comes a heavy downdraft of warm air, followed by the ground-rattling landing of an elder drake. He spits out a concerned-looking Inox, who drips from the experience of riding in the winged creature's mouth.\n\n\"Hello mercenaries! I have been searching for you for hours. This Inox,\" the drake pushes the terrified man forward. \"This Inox broke into my home and tore a scale right off my tail. Dreadful manners.\" The Inox begins to sob. \"But he told me he was something of a smith, so I gave him the opportunity to make a gift for you. Go on, show them. GO!\" The drake bellows, and the Inox jumps into action, presenting you with an impressive piece of chest armor fashioned from the lost scale.\n\nThe Inox whispers as he hands it off, trembling, \"Please. Please help me!\"\n\nThe drake continues, \"Good, well, that's settled, then. Thank you, offensive Inox. Time for me to eat you, then all will be forgiven.\"","o":[{"l":"A","t":"Let the drake eat the Inox."},{"l":"B","t":"Intercede on the Inox's behalf."}]},
    33: {"n":"\"It is you! I have heard the rumors that you forged the Dawnblade!\" Blocking your way forward on the road is a joyous old woman. \"When its existence was finally felt, we knew destiny had spoken, and it was time to fashion its legendary complement: the Duskbrand.\"\n\nShe unwraps a parcel, revealing a dark blade. Smoke seems to play across its surface, but it has a refined elegance. She hands it to you, tears in her eyes.\n\n\"My husband cast himself into the forge to get the flames hot enough—his life energy was the missing spirit needed to craft the Duskbrand, the mirror-match to the mythic Dawnblade. Finally, our family's centuries-old legacy can rest. To think, you slew the dagger-bearers, you caught the Immortal Brummix, you bested the Elder Riverwatcher, you tamed the storm in the mists. You are the chosen!\" She falls to her knees, prostrating herself in front of you.\n\nWait, uh... you didn't do any of those things. You just grabbed a sword forged by your barmate Xain.","o":[{"l":"A","t":"Graciously accept the sword."},{"l":"B","t":"Explain that she's mistaking you for someone else."}]},
    34: {"n":"\"Can it be true? The chosen are before us!\" A group of incredulous ascetic Vermlings catch up to you on the road out of Gloomhaven. \"We were told that you possessed both the mythic Dawnblade and its mirror-equal, the legendary Duskbrand. Is it true?\"\n\nAnother Vermling, the fur of his head shaved in an intricate pattern, speaks up: \"Tell us! Do you carry light and darkness in your scabbard?\"\n\nA particularly scrawny Vermling squeaks with excitement. \"Show us, please!\"","o":[{"l":"A","t":"Show off the Dawnblade and Duskbrand, as requested (requires the party have both 117 and 118)."},{"l":"B","t":"Explain that you have them elsewhere, for safekeeping."}]},
    35: {"n":"You find a familiar-looking Orchid on the road an hour outside of Gloomhaven. \"Oh hey, friends, it's you!\" Xain, a regular at the Sleeping Lion who always seems to wind up in trouble, has a bindle full of assorted possessions thrown over his shoulder. \"Oh, the bag? I'm temporarily relocating, change of scenery. Going to a tiny outpost in the frozen north.\" Xain sees your faces.\n\n\"I hear it's all kinds of nice. Very cosmopolitan, they've got everything. Snow. Even mail, sometimes. And I hear some Algox are... uh, friendly?\" He's not terribly convincing. \"Anyway, migrating also comes with the bonus of avoiding a friend of mine, who I happen to owe a little bit of money.\"\n\nXain looks uncomfortable with what he's shared. \"Whelp, best be off, no time to waste before the pass closes. It'd uh... probably be for the best if my little relocation didn't make it back to Bonecruncher, so let's just keep that between us, huh?\"","o":[{"l":"A","t":"Demand payment for your silence."},{"l":"B","t":"Ask where he got the blueprint for the Dawnblade that was forged."}]},
    36: {"n":"You slow your pace through the wilderness as you suddenly realize the animals have fallen silent, and the area around you has turned strange: the ground crumbles like sand beneath your boots, and the leaves of the nearby trees have faded to gray.\n\nYou notice a stale wind whistling from somewhere nearby, and you follow the sound to a clearing that stops your breath with awe.\n\nIt's like a charcoal sketch. Everything is a perfect pallid gray, all the color drained from the area as the entire clearing gradually turns to ash. The only color you see comes from the interplanar rift floating in midair, which dances with chromatic energy as the colors of the wilderness drain slowly into its maw.\n\nBy now, you've learned to recognize elemental magic. Somehow, the elemental energy from this plane is flowing through that rift into another realm, sucking dry the elemental essence of the trees, the earth—even the wind itself. In fact, you realize the colors have begun to fade from your own gear as well.","o":[{"l":"A","t":"Sate the rift's appetite with elemental energy of your own."},{"l":"B","t":"Enter the rift to investigate."}]},
    37: {"n":"Barreling down the road comes a red-trimmed wagon drawn by a pack of shambling skeletons. Two glum-looking Inox march behind it, keeping an eye out for any threats. As soon as you're within yelling distance, you hear the dramatic cry of \"Halt!\"\n\nOut from the wagon steps the elegant Jekserah. \"Mercenaries,\" she smiles a greeting. \"What ideal timing. You two,\" she points at the Inox, \"stop that! You don't need to water the skeletons! Just keep them leashed.\" On edge, Jekserah walks a bit down the road with you out of earshot of her twin Inox bodyguards.\n\n\"I have an army of skeletons, and somehow those two are the boneheads. And lately, I've found them quite... spineless. Questioning, almost threatening. Let alone the cost,\" she laughs with a flourish, as though this is relatable. \"In any case, it's fortuitous to see you. Walk them off into the woods and make sure they don't come back... or better yet, just bring back the bodies.\" She sighs, looking at them from a distance. \"They do have good bodies, after all.\"","o":[{"l":"A","t":"Agree to eliminate Jekserah's bodyguards."},{"l":"B","t":"Refuse to get involved."}]},
    38: {"n":"The sun has just set, and the rosy patterns in the sky are giving you a few dwindling minutes of light before you need to set camp for the night. An inviting clearing just ahead sets your mind at ease, and the small stream nearby provides a calm, quiet burbling. It's really the perfect spot to settle in until morning... perhaps a little too perfect.\n\nYou see the shadow cast by a figure a fraction of a second too late. Salt, drawn in meticulous lines across the mossy ground, forms a perfect summoning sigil. With a wave of the caster's arm, the inscription alights, completing the planar request and binding you within the area.\n\n\"Mercenaries! You have crossed the demons! Such action is punishable by death!\" A melodramatic voice rings out, followed by a cackle. \"Let your final moments be borne of the chaos that consumes you!\"","o":[{"l":"A","t":"Explain that you've changed, and you mean the demons no harm."},{"l":"B","t":"No use wasting words, it's time to cut these monsters down.","r":1}]},
    39: {"n":"The mossy trail reveals clusters of bugs at regular intervals, spread out across the forest floor. They instinctively shy away as you approach, but among their numbers, you spot a few of the peculiar bugs that are so common within the Plagueherald.\n\nA Harrower can unwind the humanoid figure it typically presents as, stretching over a wide area. Its consciousness is likewise diluted, though, and approaching one in that state can be startling or rude. It looks like there's far too many bugs to be a Harrower, though...","o":[{"l":"A","t":"Call out to the bugs."},{"l":"B","t":"Quietly walk through the thicket without alarming the insects."}]},
    40: {"n":"The road winds through a grove of trees, its saplings stretching skyward as the old growth gives much-needed comfort from the heavy sun. A lumberjack slams his axe deep into the bark of an elkwood tree, the sound ringing through the valley. A small log cabin is nearing completion, the roof partially erected. The Orchid takes a swig of water from a canteen as he watches you approach. He sums you up and rolls his eyes, chuckling.\n\n\"Mercenaries, right? I can't believe someone would pay to have you sorry saps do their bidding.\" He hefts his axe confidently. \"You wouldn't last a day out here if you actually had to work for a living. Go on, scurry off to whatever implausible scenario commands your attention today.\" He drops his voice, imitating the way you'd speak to a baby. \"I'm sure it's very important.\"","o":[{"l":"A","t":"Insult the Orchid back."},{"l":"B","t":"Challenge him to a logging competition"},{"l":"C","t":"Murder the Orchid and burn down his cabin."}]},
    41: {"n":"The shimmering, crystalline hand of an Orchid grabs at your ankle, rooting you in place. But this is no Orchid—it's \"Crystal Rot,\" the discarded corpse of an Orchid given new life through nearby necrotic energy. The patchwork of dead grass and skeletal remains of forest fauna nearby suggest this one is still in the process of forming, feeding off the death in the area.\n\nGiven it's not yet a threat, you dig it up for a better look. Sure enough, the façade is that of an Orchid: crystals dance in fractal patterns across its skin. But in the gashes between flesh, you can see the thick black ichor that has taken up residence, recoiling from the sunlight.\n\nHollowed-out eyes shift and come to rest on you, expressionless. Its mouth lilts open, testing the jaw of its deceased Orchid puppet.","o":[{"l":"A","t":"Kill the monster before it finishes forming itself."},{"l":"B","t":"Attempt to pacify the monster's anger."}]},
    42: {"n":"There were certainly heavy rains last night, but you didn't realize the full extent until you came face-to-face with a river raging right through the middle of the road.\n\nThis river is no small matter, either—even a talented jump would barely clear the water. There is no practical way around, and judging by the drizzle that began falling minutes ago, it looks like even more rain is on the way.","o":[{"l":"A","t":"Attempt to cross the river."},{"l":"B","t":"Attempt to build a bridge across."}]},
    43: {"n":"Your route takes you across a number of small streams, each of which are simple enough to ford. The one ahead of you presents a unique issue, though: whereas all of the tributaries you've crossed flow as you would expect, this larger one, paradoxically, is winding its way straight up the gentle hillside, totally unfettered by gravity.\n\nIt's worth the detour if only to appease your curiosity. You follow it up to find the stream is filling a serene pool with crisp, cool water. The edges of basin are meticulously chiseled rock, and peering in past the ripples... wait, is that a Lurker down there? No... it's a person! You can see them resting at the bottom of the pool, motionless in a state of repose.","o":[{"l":"A","t":"Try to get the body out of the water."},{"l":"B","t":"Your curiosity is satisfied—it's time to move on.","r":1}]},
    44: {"n":"A splitting headache. Nausea.\n\nYou feel the crackling pulse of an energy rift looming behind you. You turn to face it, and sure enough, a ring of blue energy, large enough to step through, hangs impossibly in the air. You peer through and see a landscape not dissimilar to your own. Contemplating it could keep you busy for hours, but it feels familiar somehow, like a persistent dream.\n\nNo, not a dream—a challenge. Do you have the fortitude to step through into the unknown?","o":[{"l":"A","t":"Enter the rift. (Each character starts the next scenario with Light 1. Flip this card.)"},{"l":"B","t":"Refuse the call."}]},
    45: {"n":"The sound of wailing wakes you from an otherwise peaceful rest along the trail. You follow the sound to a pool of glowing water at the base of a willowburn tree. When you get to the water's edge, a ghostly, translucent woman appears, knee-deep in the swirling current, weeping into her hands. She doesn't acknowledge you, lost in her own thoughts.\n\nYou reach out to console her when someone slaps your hand away—an old man with a cane wards you back with a \"Tisk tisk.\" The woman continues to sob.\n\nHe sits down on a log, speaking plainly and unconcerned if she overhears. \"That's not a woman. Best not to touch it, she'll wrap you up in her darkness, and you'll fall to pieces. She's been in that pool for as long as I've been alive, weeping and pulling the unsuspecting close. I've stopped a good many, but she's nabbed her fair share.\"\n\nHe pulls out some fruit and continues between bites. \"She'll be gone by morning. Shows up once a month or so. Good news is that when she's gone, the water will keep glowing for a few days, puts some pep in your step if you drink it.\"","o":[{"l":"A","t":"Reach out to the woman."},{"l":"B","t":"Wait until morning and try the water.","r":1}]},
    46: {"n":"Your travels between two distant hills take you past signage of an Inox clan claiming ownership of the valley. The warning permits peaceful passage, but claims dire punishment for any who would stay past dark. Unfortunately, it's already late in the day, and with no other option, you make camp and start a fire.\n\nAs you're about to enter your tent, echoes of voices waft over the hills, and the faint, low sound is chilling. It may be the wind, or it may be some inhuman force.","o":[{"l":"A","t":"Quickly put out the fire and sneak out into the brush to get a look."},{"l":"B","t":"Wait by the fire for whatever comes."}]},
    47: {"n":"You encounter two Valraths with a small horse and cart on the side of the road. They seem uncomfortable at your approach, their hands instinctively going for their weapons.\n\nYou make a sign of peace and they relax a little.\n\n\"Perhaps it is good that you happen by, travelers,\" the younger one says. \"Our horse is completely spent and we fare little better. We are in desperate need of some supplies to finish our journey.\"\n\nThe Valrath offers a sorry smile. \"Do you think you could help us a bit?\"","o":[{"l":"A","t":"Give the Valraths a portion of your supplies."},{"l":"B","t":"You spent good money on these supplies. Refuse to help."}]},
    48: {"n":"You hear the labored moans of an animal slowly dying nearby. In the shade of a tall outcropping, you find a Quatryl farmer kneeling beside a kivak, a horned beast of burden, trying to console it as it passes. He sees you approach and wipes a tear away.\n\n\"This is my poor kivak. Best companion I could've ever had for darn near fifty years now. She stumbled and took a battering coming down this cliffside.\" He points at the deep gouge in the cliffside that traces where she fell. A pool of blood has started to form under the animal, and it's clear it's in pain. The farmer looks absolutely distraught, stroking the beast's head as he tries to comfort it.","o":[{"l":"A","t":"Offer to put it out of its misery for the farmer."},{"l":"B","t":"Attempt to heal the beast."}]},
    49: {"n":"You find a sobbing Quatryl sitting dejectedly by the side of the road. His attire is ostentatious—it would be extravagant even for the high-society galas of White Oak. He pulls a foppish hat off his head, blowing his nose dramatically into it as you approach. \"Oh, sadness and woe! Hasten away, travelers, lest you learn the sorry tale of Basselhoff, the greatest bard in the world!\n\n\"Stifle your primal urge to secure my autograph, as I would be unable to command even a quill to fulfill your desire, so distraught is my current countenance! I have written a ballad so flawless even mine own consummate mind cannot comprehend the coda. Behold!\"\n\n\"A life of woe befell my mate,\n\nFrom hard-won wound to hard-lost gait,\n\nA lass one day did end his strife...\"\n\nBasselhoff wilts, unable to finish. \"I have... writer's block!\" He curls into a ball at your feet, weeping. Offer him a final line to finish the song:","o":[{"l":"A","t":"\"A doctor who became his wife.\""},{"l":"B","t":"\"A bandit with a hunting knife.\""},{"l":"C","t":"\"And then she died.\""}]},
    50: {"n":"Pfhisss. Pf-Pff-Pffffhisss! Against the afternoon sun, you can barely make them out, but tiny, molten rocks are pelting the landscape around you. This meteorological anomaly increases in intensity—when trees begin to fall, you take cover under a rocky outcropping. A moment later, a merchant husband and wife take cover under the same outcropping, clothes singed and worse for wear.\n\n\"This is awful! Our whole camp is still out there!\"\n\n\"Our kivak won't be able to survive, and I left him leashed to the tent! Is there any chance you could—\"\n\n\"Oh, the tent! That was expensive! If you could help—\"\n\n\"And dinner! We left our dinner on the campfire!\"\n\n\"Actually, there's a couple other things, if you're already going out there—\"\n\n\"—wait, where's our son?\"","o":[{"l":"A","t":"Choose which of the following requests you will retrieve for the couple, then flip this card. Each will require a separate trip: their child, their Kivak, their tent, their box of animal pelts, their trunk of personal effects, their dinner and accompanying serving set, and a lovely Orchid-woven rug they had spread under the campsite."}]},
    51: {"n":"\"Hello travelers!\" You're a day's walk from Gloomhaven when you come upon a curious sight: a roadside stand stocked with nothing but acorns. The proprietor sees your confusion and she gives a polite smile.\n\n\"These are not just any acorns—they're the seeds of none other than Gloomhaven's Great Oak! Each is endowed with a bit of the grace that makes the Oak so powerful.\" She pulls out a tray of additional acorns that have a darkened, antique feel. \"And if you want some exceptional potency, I can also offer acorns from White Oak's Great Oak—the original! There's nothing luckier you could carry with you.\"\n\nWhether they work or not, this woman is clearly a true believer.","o":[{"l":"A","t":"Purchase some Gloomhaven Great Oak acorns (requires 10 gold).","r":1},{"l":"B","t":"Purchase some White Oak Great Oak acorns (requires 15 gold).","r":1},{"l":"C","t":"You're not interested in carrying an acorn in your pocket.","r":1}]},
    52: {"n":"Most travelers along the East Road keep to themselves, never certain whose day they might be disturbing. That common courtesy is left by the roadside for the numerous panhandlers and roving salespeople accosting anyone who might have some coin. You watch a Vermling tell off a party of well-dressed travelers within earshot ahead of you.\n\n\"You think fancy talk and fancy learning means you can walk over others, eh? Mother always said there's worse than being stupid.\" Kicking their wagon as they hustle on, the scraggly Vermling approaches you with her sack. \"How about you? A potion for the big, strong adventurers!\" she says as she waves you down. \"Helps in battle, best quality.\"\n\nYou are about to push forward when she pulls the potion out of her sack. It glows silver in the daylight—an impressive, large flask of master craftsmanship. She pulls the wooden stopper and takes a small sip. The enchanted container sets immediately to work regenerating the removed liquid.\n\n\"Mother always said, 'Seeing is buying.' You want it?\"","o":[{"l":"A","t":"Agree to pay 15 gold for the potion."},{"l":"B","t":"Haggle with the Vermling for the potion at a good price."}]},
    53: {"n":"\"Hard times, hard times, that's all. Sometimes things're going up, sometimes things're going sideways. I'm in something of a lateral move at the moment, myself.\" The old man dusts himself off and looks up with a wink. \"But you can never be poor if you've got secrets, right? Secrets always have value.\n\n\"Tell you what: you look like trustworthy folks. I'll tell you about a secret too far for my old legs to reach.\" He grunts and something gives a pop in his back. You can't tell if his face shows relief or pain, but he continues without acknowledging it. \"You go gather it, and then you split the treasure with me, however you think's right. Couldn't be fairer, eh?\"","o":[{"l":"A","t":"Agree to quest for the old man, and split the loot when you've determined the difficulty."},{"l":"B","t":"Demand an even split now."}]},
    54: {"n":"Words have power. Ever since you raised the Bladeswarm from the sands of time, you have had a heightened sense of the world around you. Each crawling entity, each fly buzzing by in the air: are they part of some secret network, waiting for the code to unlock them from their state? What else could be waiting for the latent command to spring to life?\n\nA few hours outside Gloomhaven, you feel the instinctual pull of something. You stop, looking at the world around you. A beetle runs across your boot, and you know then and there that something is waiting for your call.\n\nIf \"Bladeswarm\" could summon a Harrower, perhaps other combinations of letters could do the same.","o":[{"l":"A","t":"Make camp, meditate, and wait for a sign."},{"l":"B","t":"Spout random sounds in the ancient tongues.","r":1}]},
    55: {"n":"Words have power. Ever since you raised the Bladeswarm from the sands of time, you have had a heightened sense of the world around you. Each crawling entity, each fly buzzing by in the air: are they part of some secret network, waiting for the code to unlock them from their state? What else could be waiting for the latent command to spring to life?\n\nA few hours outside Gloomhaven, you feel the instinctual pull of something. This feels stronger than it was before... you feel you've found the right place for your beckoning. But what to say?","o":[{"l":"A","t":"Meditate and wait for a sign.","r":1},{"l":"B","t":"Speak from the heart."}]},
    56: {"n":"An excited Quatryl flags you down as you approach a roadside stand. \"Hello! I've invented the most highly advanced form of personal protection ever conceived—the negativity pump. I'm looking for field testers.\" An Inox carries out an unusual system of tubes and gizmos along with a yellow shield. Wait, is that—yes, it's your old friend the Bruiser working as a display model!\n\nBefore you can say hello, the Quatryl grabs the dagger. \"It's demonstration time!\" The Quatryl stabs it towards the yellow shield held by your old friend. It's a pitiful miss. The Quatryl stabs again and again, increasing in accuracy until his small frame manages to punch right through the shield! The Bruiser winces a bit, but then smiles an unconvincing smile.\n\nThe Quatryl looks pleased, then rushes off to get paperwork. The Bruiser takes the opportunity alone to level with you: \"Listen, this thing is junk. Dangerous junk. But I get a commission for signups, uh, could you...\"","o":[{"l":"A","t":"Agree to test a single unit."},{"l":"B","t":"Have the entire party test the units."}]},
    57: {"n":"A hearty smell permeates the forest: a mix of fresh moss and old-growth trees. As you descend towards a streambank, your footfalls become more pronounced, and upon inspection you find that the carpet of greenery underfoot has been intentionally placed across a bed of crunchy dried leaves. Is this some kind of natural alarm system? You draw your weapons and prepare for a fight.\n\nBefore you have a chance to ponder it further, you spot a haggard woman on the path before you. She is inscrutably old and eyes you with a mix of curiosity and concern. A rope necklace entwined with tree bark hangs low across her torso.\n\nThe ground beneath you crinkles like autumn leaves and vines begin to root you in place.","o":[{"l":"A","t":"Cut yourself free of the vines."},{"l":"B","t":"Attack the woman."},{"l":"C","t":"Drop your weapons."}]},
    58: {"n":"The morning tranquility is suddenly disturbed by a green-clad familiar face tumbling out of the underbrush. Your old friend the Silent Knife dusts themselves off, panting as they scan the horizon. They cast a furtive look over their shoulder before they finally spot you.\n\n\"Is that? It is!\" You're not sure if it's relief or the formulation of a plan playing across their face. \"Perfect timing, as always. Here, take this.\"\n\nGain \"Major Element Potion\" 045.\n\n\"Hang onto that, I'll come back for it.\" And with that, they take off at a dead sprint towards Gloomhaven.\n\nA moment later, a guard steps out of the underbrush.\n\n\"You there! Did you see an assassin pass by here? They murdered an alchemist and made off with their potions!\" You reflexively push the potion deeper into your satchel, although he narrows his eyes nearly imperceptibly.","o":[{"l":"A","t":"Turn over the potion and point him in the direction of the Silent Knife."},{"l":"B","t":"Try to throw him off the Silent Knife's trail."},{"l":"C","t":"It's just like the Silent Knife to leave you to clean up their mess. Kill the guard."}]},
    59: {"n":"After an early start out the city gates this morning, you're still clustered with a group of early riser travelers. Each of you is trying to give some separation, but you're awkwardly all traveling the same direction, so it may be hours before you stop staring at the back of their heads.\n\nThe problem quickly solves itself, though: riding up on horses, a band of rough men overtake and surround your group. They look grim and dangerous, and you immediately pull out your weapons as they approach. As the encounter is about to come to blows, you hear a familiar laugh—it's the Silent Knife, riding with these miscreants.","o":[{"l":"A","t":"Call out to the Silent Knife."},{"l":"B","t":"Attack. Friend or not, those who prey on the weak should be brought to justice."}]},
    60: {"n":"\"Thanks for your help with that guard.\" Up ahead, leaning against a tree, is your old friend the Silent Knife. They're referring to you covering for their recent hit and theft of an element potion. \"It was all a big misunderstanding, really, and I could've talked my way out of it I'm sure, but I appreciate your help all the same.\"\n\nThe Silent Knife gives a winning smile. \"So, as promised, I'm back for it. Hand it over, would you?\"","o":[{"l":"A","t":"Give the Silent Knife the potion (requires \"\" 45)."},{"l":"B","t":"Refuse to give the Silent Knife the potion back."}]},
    61: {"n":"Before you is an impossible sight: a giant mechanical man leaning against the mountainside. Were it not seated, it would be taller than the surrounding trees. Rust and moss have built up along its jaw and chest, and you'd assume it last moved hundreds of years ago. Suddenly, the mouth creaks open.\n\n\"Halt! Stop right there, or I will defeat you!\" The iron machine's mouth swings open and shut, the unfortunate sound of metal grinding against metal punctuating each word. \"Such is the decree of the Automatic King!\" It would be quite the feat for this creature to rise, even if it were unfettered by the forest undergrowth holding it down. You hesitantly approach.\n\n\"Halt! Kneel and swear to be my knight!\" Suddenly, the chest cavity swings open, and from the darkness, a poorly-aimed arrow fires out. You think you catch a glimpse of some small figures giggling inside before it swings back shut.\n\n\"Kneel and accept the rule of the Automatic King!\"","o":[{"l":"A","t":"Play along and pledge fealty to the Automatic King."},{"l":"B","t":"Refuse and search the ruined automaton for valuables."}]},
    62: {"n":"Taking shelter from the rain, you set up camp beneath the boughs of a sleepy willowburn tree. It's an idyllic spot, and even the slow drip of water off the treetops has a calming effect.\n\nWhen the rain finally subsides, you spot something exposed by the runoff: the edge of a crystal protruding from the soil. You carefully dig it out to find what looks like discarded crystalline growths of at least two different Orchids, although you don't see any nearby belongings.","o":[{"l":"A","t":"Bury the crystals back under the soil."},{"l":"B","t":"Dig up the crystals and take them with you."}]},
    63: {"n":"You hear the distinctive sound of metal-on-metal in the distance and race up a hill beside the road to investigate.\n\nIn full, antagonistic glory, you see a familiar Inox in heated battle with a group of Gloomhaven guards. Though outnumbered and bloodied, you've never seen that stop the Berserker before. The guards get a deep blow against your former companion and the Berserker flies into a rage, cleaving off limbs with the ease of cutting grass.","o":[{"l":"A","t":"Assist the Berserker against the guards."},{"l":"B","t":"Assist the guards against the Berserker."}]},
    64: {"n":"An ostentatious wagon passes you on the worn road, pulled by six gleaming-white horses. You hear a confused voice inside followed by the scrambling of reins as the wagon comes to a rapid halt. Out pops a familiar face: it's the Berserker!\n\n\"Friends! It is you! Please, come aboard, I'll happily take you wherever you're going.\" You climb aboard, and the bumpy, dusty road all but vanishes amidst the plush seats.\n\nThe Berserker sees your surprise. \"I came into some money after a fight. Who knew fighting could make you good money? I guess it's about fighting the right people.\" You lean back and relax thanks to your friend's good fortune, half-dozing on the way.\n\nYou wake up an hour later with a bit of a start, as the pleasant trip is interrupted by a sticky sensation under your boot-heel. You look under the seat and see a bloody axe embedded in a merchant's corpse... likely the former owner of this very wagon.","o":[{"l":"A","t":"Continue the ride without comment."},{"l":"B","t":"Raise the issue with the Berserker."}]},
    65: {"n":"The silence along the road gives you an itchy sense of foreboding. You know someone is watching you, but your attempts to spot from where are fruitless. Finally, you give up and call out to the surrounding trees. To your surprise, they answer.\n\n\"You are the ones who fought beside the bow-wielding Orchid, is this true?\" A Vermling steps out of the undergrowth, eyeing you cautiously. \"A group of wild-looking Orchids approached us in our woods. They requested rights to trade with us. To purchase our knowledge.\" The Vermling leans on a gnarled staff and scratches at a long, scabbed-over gash running from his ear to his neckline.\n\n\"We are always wary of traps and betrayal out here on the edge of the wilds. We have seen your actions, watched your successes. If you think these Orchids can be trusted, then we will open trade with them. If you do not, we will hunt them for their deception.\"","o":[{"l":"A","t":"Vouch for the Orchids."},{"l":"B","t":"Advise against trading with the Orchids."}]},
    66: {"n":"You feel a searing heat at the back of your neck, and a sense of foreboding overwhelms your consciousness. You crouch and survey the area, but there's no threat to be seen. Still, you can't shake the feeling of camouflaged hidden danger.\n\n\"Halt!\" The voice rings out amongst the trees, coming from everywhere and nowhere at once. \"You have trespassed on the land of the forest Orchids. Return from whence you came, or perish!\" A rain of arrows peppers the ground around you to emphasize the point. \"There are no exceptions. The next arrow will be a kill shot!\"\n\nYou need to take cover from this murderous stalker, but when you try to stand, the crushing feeling down your spine deepens.","o":[{"l":"A","t":"Ask for safe passage."},{"l":"B","t":"Press forward."},{"l":"C","t":"Retreat."}]},
    67: {"n":"\"Terrible. Wretched, sloppy. This is what mercenaries look like today? Feh! I can't believe my old trainee the Elementalist spent time with you.\"\n\nAn old Savvas parked by the side of the road reviews your party with disgust. \"Yes, I know exactly who you are. My trainee said you were rough around the edges, but this? Back in my day, you'd never see such miserable presentation. Where're your uniforms? People pay to hire you like this? I could take you all on with an arm tied behind my back!\" It shakes its head.\n\n\"How about this? I was able to whip that Elementalist into shape long before they joined you, and I can probably do something with the sorry state you're in. I'll give you a few hours of my time, you give me some coin for my trouble.\"","o":[{"l":"A","t":"Pay the old Savvas for training (requires 5xC collective gold)."},{"l":"B","t":"This insulting Savvas needs to be put in its place. Challenge it to a fight."},{"l":"C","t":"Since it thinks it's so great, take it up on its offer to fight you with one arm tied behind its back."}]},
    68: {"n":"A rustling outside your tent in the early morning pushes you to a groggy state of alert. Emerging from the tent, you find merely a lost kivak, the traditional steed of the Savvas peoples. These stout, horned beasts of burden have been domesticated over time, and are typically found only with someone keeping them in tow. Perhaps this one is lost?\n\nYou survey the creature as it munches a sprig of grass in the moonlight. It takes a few steps, and you can see it favoring its back left heel. Closer investigation reveals a deep cut clear to its anklebone. Thankfully, the kivak doesn't look too concerned by it.\n\nAgainst the struggling of a very large, very stubborn beast, you manage to bandage the injury. Satisfied it's contributed enough, the kivak curls up and shuts its eyes, dozing in the warmth of the morning sun.","o":[{"l":"A","t":"Try to find its owner."},{"l":"B","t":"A kivak can be useful—bring it along with you."}]},
    69: {"n":"\"Travelers! Ho there! If it isn't the fine folks that brought my kivak back to me.\" You smell the herd before you see them: a thick curtain of farm-beast odor settles across your face, coating your tongue as you approach. You greet the shepherd and try your best to suppress the taste.\n\n\"Would you care to take one of these beauties along with you? They like seeing the world, and if they get too spooked, they know to just run home to me. Fancy a friend? You're family now, so you'll get family pricing.\"","o":[{"l":"A","t":"Rent a kivak.","r":1},{"l":"B","t":"You're not interested in a rental today, but you'd like to say hello to the kivak named Grump.","r":1}]},
    70: {"n":"An explosive crack shakes you out of your tent, sending you scrambling for your weapons in the moonlight. When the adrenaline wears down, you spot a fissure at waist height carved into the air itself. Pouring out is a steady stream of water that pools on the ground below it. The disregard of physics tells you it's a rip between planes.\n\nYou pull tentatively at the edges and it opens further. Out splashes a unique jellyfish—the kind you remember your old friend the Soultether using. While you don't speak jellyfish, it looks as confused as you are.\n\nThat's when you spot it: clutched between the jellyfish's tentacles is a torn patch of the Soultether's garments. This is no random occurrence... it's a distress signal.","o":[{"l":"A","t":"Abandon your current mission to come to the Soultether's aid right now."},{"l":"B","t":"Note the location of the rift and return in the future."}]},
    71: {"n":"The sun has just finished painting the sky brilliant orange hues, fading to the dusk of twilight. The stars do little to illuminate your path, and you're just about to settle down for the evening when a familiar voice rings out from the darkness.\n\n\"The thread of spaces between spaces. Stars embroidered in the dark. A tapestry unwound.\" It is your old friend the Nightshroud, although they sound less coherent than you remember.\n\nThey shift their weight, and a hole in the air beside them follows where they stood. The Nightshroud touches the edges with curiosity, as though seeing it all for the first time. Their eyes snap into focus, looking at you with intensity.\n\n\"Step through and I will take you. We will journey together again. You won't mind the cost, your mind.\"","o":[{"l":"A","t":"Step through the rift."},{"l":"B","t":"Thank the Nightshroud, but decide to walk to your destination instead."}]},
    72: {"n":"You smell the corpses before you see them. A group of five bodies spread across the road, flies buzzing around them in a frenzy.\n\nAs you get closer, you see the wretched, deformed looks on their faces and the boils covering their skin. They've been here for some time, and their patchwork of equipment suggests their former occupation: likely a group of cutthroats.\n\nFrom their positions, you deduce they were engaged in battle when they suddenly succumbed to some horrible disease.\n\nYou can think of at least one way that could have happened: fine handiwork from the Plagueherald.","o":[{"l":"A","t":"Leave the corpses alone."},{"l":"B","t":"Inspect the corpses for valuables."},{"l":"C","t":"Burn the bodies."}]},
    73: {"n":"\"Well, if it isn't those resilient mercenaries that tested so much of my equipment!\" By the side of the road is your old traveling companion, the Quartermaster. They've set up a sturdy stand displaying all manner of quality adventuring equipment and seem to be earning a healthy living catering to a very niche demographic.\n\n\"Who better to sell to mercenaries than a former mercenary? I'd rather sell to them than do the work myself. This is where the real money is. Every fresh-faced upstart will need an iron helmet or leather armor someday.\"\n\nYou take a look through the wares.","o":[{"l":"A","t":"Ask if the Quartermaster has any spare supplies, for old times' sake.","r":1},{"l":"B","t":"Purchase something from the Quartermaster's shop.","r":1}]},
    74: {"n":"A Quatryl in a foppish hat is standing in the road, seemingly waiting for you. He sweeps his leg back into a low, formal bow as you approach. With a snap, he returns to full height to address you.\n\n\"Congratulations, you have earned my esteemed accompaniment. I, Basseloff, the greatest bard in the world, am in express need of adventure to top off the tanks of mirth that fuel my passionate ballads.\" You are dreading how this conversation ends.\n\n\"As you are held in some regard, albeit lesser to mine own, I have selected you as fitting companions for this task. I will support your quest through the incomparable power of my verse. Be not alarmed, I have already deemed it appropriate to waive my customary 100 gold fee for the privilege of my esteemed support, so this adventure will be exclusively without cost to you.\" He doesn't seem to notice the emotional cost of keeping this blathering Quatryl alive.","o":[{"l":"A","t":"Accept Basseloff's \"help\"."},{"l":"B","t":"You don't want to babysit—refuse the Quatryl's assistance."}]},
    75: {"n":"Before you, nearly dead-center in the road, is a very conspicuously placed bear trap. You can see the contours of the metal jaws, and the trigger mechanism is barely covered by a single, broad leaf. It's no surprise an animal hasn't been caught yet, and it would take an absolute fool to step onto it.","o":[{"l":"A","t":"Walk around the trap."},{"l":"B","t":"Step on the trap. (Choose one character to spring it.)"}]},
    76: {"n":"A buzzing insect swoops around you, then flies off. A few minutes later, you find your old companion the Bladeswarm in the middle of the road. For a creature as timeless as it is, it wastes no time in greeting.\n\n\"We have 'retired' many times before, and always new threats draw us from our slumber.\" They pause dramatically, but when it's clear you don't have a response, they continue. \"The many peoples will fight over the dirt they call home, but there is a larger threat, buried beneath the dirt. Beneath the bones, beneath the sands, beneath all creation. It waits.\"\n\nIt sees you're not comprehending. \"No matter. You must survive the tasks today that you may fight the battles tomorrow. Tell me, should I assist you defensively or offensively?\"","o":[{"l":"A","t":"Ask for a defensive gift."},{"l":"B","t":"Ask for an offensive gift."}]},
    77: {"n":"A lone hristek stands beside the road, basking in the bright sun. It's carrying several packs of supplies, but there's no rider to be seen. It takes a moment for your eyes to adjust, and then you realize the truth all at once: there are thousands of bugs taking respite in the shade of the hristek, crawling across each other like a black puddle.\n\nAs soon as you take notice, it begins to draw itself up into a Harrower—and it's not until it dons a familiar helm that you realize it's your old companion, the Bladeswarm!\n\n\"Greetings. This creature is assisting in transport of my belongings. The breed is strong—they have sturdy backs and are full of blood inside their skin.\" The Bladeswarm falls silent, stroking the hristek. After a moment, it snaps its attention back to you and abruptly continues. \"I am in search of liquid. If you can provide, I can compensate.\"","o":[{"l":"A","t":"Sell the Bladeswarm potions.","r":1},{"l":"B","t":"You need your equipment for your mission and have nothing to spare.","r":1}]},
  },
};
